{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"statements\": [\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-04\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion of second-quarter revenue and $63.73 billion of operating income, an operating margin of about 66%, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company-reported quarterly revenue\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n     \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"about 66%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"derived from reported revenue and operating income\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S1\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's latest reported quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in total revenue, so roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI, according to GuruFocus's recounting of the results in its 22 September 2026 article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Data-center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Data-center segment revenue, as reported in the company's quarterly results\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company-wide revenue\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Data-center share of total sales\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"roughly 92.5%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Data-center revenue divided by total revenue, as calculated by GuruFocus\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 16,\n   \"ref\": \"S2\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company gross margin guidance, lowered on higher-than-expected memory costs and helped by price increases already executed\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S3\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA said on Monday 28 September 2026 that it authorized an additional $150 billion for its share repurchase program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion, which it described as the largest increase in a share repurchase authorization in history; the company said it expects to complete the total remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"additional share repurchase authorization\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Additional authorization; takes total buyback authorization to $235 billion, expected to be completed through fiscal 2028.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"total share repurchase authorization\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Total program after the additional $150 billion authorization.\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 32,\n   \"ref\": \"S4\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA has a $150 billion share buyback plan, described by Yahoo Finance as the largest ever, which the article cites alongside its cash generation as justification for the repurchase programme.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share buyback plan\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"announced repurchase authorisation; described as the largest ever\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S5\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue growth of 85% year over year, with Data Center revenues up 92% year over year, as retold by Zacks Equity Research in its Analyst Blog highlights.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"85%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"year over year, company total\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Data Center revenues growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"92%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"year over year, Data Center segment\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S6\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"74%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company guided, down from fiscal Q2 2027\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"75%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"expected bottom\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 6,\n   \"ref\": \"S7\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA says its $108 billion revenue forecast for the current quarter assumes no China data-center chip revenue; any China data-center chip sales would come on top of that outlook.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue forecast for the current quarter\",\n     \"period\": \"Current quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company forecast; assumes no China data-center chip revenue\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S8\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's second-quarter net income rose 126% year-on-year to $59.7bn, according to the Yahoo Finance UK article, which notes earnings grew roughly 4.5 times faster than the share price over the same comparison.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n     \"period\": \"Second quarter (year-on-year)\",\n     \"value\": \"$59.7bn\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net income growth, year-on-year\",\n     \"period\": \"Second quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"126%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S9\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 EPS of $2.22, topping the $2.09 consensus and marking its fifth consecutive beat, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 FY2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"as reported\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Consensus earnings per share\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 FY2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$2.09\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"consensus estimate\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S10\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-04\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported free cash flow of $27.02 billion in the fiscal year ended January 2024, $60.85 billion in fiscal 2025 and $96.68 billion in fiscal 2026.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year ended January 2024\",\n     \"value\": \"$27.02 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year ended January 2025\",\n     \"value\": \"$60.85 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year ended January 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$96.68 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S11\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's free cash flow fell by more than half in the quarter before the 29 September 2026 article, mostly because accounts receivable rose by more than $22 billion; the article states the sales were booked but the cash had not yet been collected.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"last quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"fell by more than half\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company change in free cash flow versus prior year quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S12\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"Fool.com reports that Nvidia's sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively, in fiscal 2026, and that two customers make up more than a third of sales — a customer concentration it flags as a risk.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Sales to a direct customer as % of total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"22%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"direct customer\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Sales to a direct customer as % of total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"14%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"direct customer\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S13\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia, in its quarterly results reported in the month before this 23 September 2026 Barchart article, posted what the article calls a market-beating fiscal Q2 and issued guidance for about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"about 70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia's own guidance as reported by Barchart; growth measure not further specified in the item\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 17,\n   \"ref\": \"S14\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-02\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin outlook\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"74%, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management outlook as reported by the article; item does not state the period covered\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S15\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA generated $24.08 billion in operating cash flow and $21.34 billion in free cash flow during fiscal Q2 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Operating cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$24.08 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$21.34 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S16\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, which the article says implies 89% growth.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal third quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately $108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company guidance; implied 89% growth is the article's calculation\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 30,\n   \"ref\": \"S17\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the Morningstar author calculates at 2,400%. The author estimates roughly $25 billion per year would be returned to shareholders through the dividend, which would make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US market; that annual figure is the author's approximation, not a company-disclosed total.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n     \"period\": \"from the May 2026 declaration\",\n     \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"quarterly payout, raised from $0.01 per share\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\",\n     \"period\": \"prior to the May 2026 declaration\",\n     \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"increase in quarterly dividend\",\n     \"period\": \"May 2026 declaration\",\n     \"value\": \"2,400%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"author's calculation\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"annual dividend paid to shareholders\",\n     \"period\": \"annual, based on the May 2026 rate\",\n     \"value\": \"$25 billion per year\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"author's approximation (\\\"Something like\\\"); total annual dividend return to shareholders\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S18\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07 (July window)\",\n   \"statement\": \"Broadcom AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion in Q2, up 143% year over year.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S19\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported free cash flow of $69.9 billion for the first half of fiscal 2027, 77% higher than a year before, or around $35 billion a quarter; CEO Jensen Huang linked the buyback increase to the company's \\\"cash generation\\\" in the announcement.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first half\",\n     \"value\": \"$69.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company reported; around $35 billion a quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first half vs a year before\",\n     \"value\": \"77%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"higher than a year before\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S20\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"Former Wall Street analyst David Bennett, writing on X.com and quoted by the article, stated that Nvidia generated $74.4 billion of operating cash flow in the first half of fiscal year 2027 alone while still returning $46.1 billion to shareholders, which he said lets the company fund AI expansion and buy back stock at the same time.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"operating cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"first half of fiscal year 2027 (through July 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$74.4 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"operating cash flow, company level\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S21\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue, year-over-year change\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company-level, year over year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 38,\n   \"ref\": \"S22\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's board approved raising the quarterly dividend from $0.01 to $0.25 per share in May 2026, a 2,400% increase that lifted the quarterly payout from about $243 million (fiscal 2027 first quarter, when the penny dividend was still in force) to approximately $6.0 billion. The first $0.25 payment went out on 26 June 2026 to holders of record as of 4 June, and a second $0.25 distribution went ex-dividend on 10 September 2026 with payment scheduled for 1 October 2026; the new annual rate is $1 per share.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n     \"period\": \"quarterly, from May 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"raised from $0.01 per share per quarter; board-approved in May\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend payout, total\",\n     \"period\": \"per quarter after the increase\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately $6.0 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"approximate, based on company disclosures and share counts at the time of payment\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend cash outlay\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"about $243 million\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"at the prior $0.01 per share quarterly rate\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S23\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-05\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA spent $20 billion on share repurchases in its most recent quarter and $40 billion in the first half of fiscal 2027, split evenly at $20 billion in each of the first and second quarters, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share repurchases (buybacks)\",\n     \"period\": \"most recent quarter (Q2 fiscal 2027)\",\n     \"value\": \"$20 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company-wide buyback spend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share repurchases (buybacks)\",\n     \"period\": \"first half of fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"$20 billion in each of the first and second quarters\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S24\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's diluted share count for its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026, was only about 1% smaller than a year before despite almost $40 billion of buybacks in the fiscal year's first half; earnings per share more than doubled.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"earnings per share growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S25\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Hopper generation)\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"roughly $18 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Blackwell generation)\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Rubin generation)\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 5,\n   \"ref\": \"S26\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S27\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"In NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027, sales to the largest cloud companies were $49 billion, up 13% from the prior quarter, while sales to all other data center customers were $40 billion, up 25% from the prior quarter.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales to the largest cloud companies\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$49 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"largest cloud company customers only\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales to the largest cloud companies, change from prior quarter\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"13%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"quarter over quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales to all other data center customers\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"data center customers other than the largest cloud companies\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales to all other data center customers, change from prior quarter\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"25%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"quarter over quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S28\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"Trefis reports that NVIDIA's operating margin — the share of revenue left after running costs — is 65%, the highest in ten years, versus a three-year average of 59% (the article does not state the exact measurement period).\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"65%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share of revenue left after running costs; highest in ten years\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"operating margin (three-year average)\",\n     \"period\": \"three-year average\",\n     \"value\": \"59%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"three-year average\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S29\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028, and management expects the higher prices to lift gross margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management expectation, helped by price increases taking effect in fiscal Q1 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S30\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"The low end of NVIDIA management's gross margin guidance for fiscal Q3 2027 is 73.5%, per the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance, low end\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"73.5%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"low end of management's gross margin guide for the quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S31\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"fool.com reports that NVIDIA generated $192.9 billion in net income over the trailing 12 months, compared with $6.4 billion for AMD, citing this in a valuation comparison of the two AI chip makers.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"net income\",\n     \"period\": \"trailing 12 months\",\n     \"value\": \"$192.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S32\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"Fool.com reports that Nvidia's fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, produced revenue of nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, net income of approximately $120.1 billion, and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the previous fiscal year.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$215.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total; up roughly 65.5% year over year\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$120.1 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"55.6%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company net margin; prior fiscal year 55.8%\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 6,\n   \"ref\": \"S33\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-02\",\n   \"statement\": \"In its latest reported quarter, Nvidia spent $19.7 billion on share buybacks and $6 billion on dividends, versus Apple's $25.9 billion of buybacks and $4 billion of dividends in Apple's fiscal 2026 third quarter; a year earlier, in the second quarter of Nvidia's fiscal 2026, Nvidia's buybacks and dividends combined totaled $10 billion.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share buybacks\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$19.7 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia share repurchases\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"dividends paid\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$6 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia dividends\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"buybacks and dividends combined\",\n     \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2026 (year-ago quarter)\",\n     \"value\": \"$10 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia share repurchases plus dividends, combined\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S34\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"about 20%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management expectation; share of data center revenue\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 5,\n   \"ref\": \"S35\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07 (July window)\",\n   \"statement\": \"Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI from late 2026 into 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S36\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA returned approximately $25.78 billion through share repurchases and dividends in fiscal Q2 2027, and in late September 2026 increased its share repurchase authorization by $150 billion, leaving a total of $235 billion available for future buybacks.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Share repurchases and dividends\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$25.78 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Approximately\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Increase in share repurchase authorization\",\n     \"period\": \"Late September 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Total share repurchase authorization available\",\n     \"period\": \"After late September 2026 increase\",\n     \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Available for future buybacks\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 4,\n   \"ref\": \"S37\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia estimates that its revenue growth for fiscal 2027 will be around 70%, according to Fool.com contributor Keithen Drury.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"FY 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"around 70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia estimate as cited for the full fiscal year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 9,\n   \"ref\": \"S38\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia management said demand for Vera Rubin currently implies its CPU revenue will more than double in fiscal 2028, which would position the company as one of the world's leading server CPU suppliers, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin CPU revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"more than double\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management statement; demand-implied\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S39\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration, per the Morningstar article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n     \"period\": \"announced at the May 2026 dividend declaration\",\n     \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"buyback authorization announced alongside the dividend increase\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 4,\n   \"ref\": \"S40\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management said Vera Rubin, its newest platform, is expected to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and put NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin versus about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$40 billion per gigawatt\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Management's estimate of NVIDIA revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, for Vera Rubin\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Management's estimate of NVIDIA revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, for Blackwell (comparison figure)\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S41\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-05\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up from $39 billion in the quarter of the lost H20 sales.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"data center segment\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"quarter of the lost H20 sales (fiscal Q1 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$39 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"data center segment\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S42\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia repurchased approximately $34 billion of its shares in fiscal year 2025, over $40.4 billion in fiscal year 2026, and $39 billion in the first half of fiscal year 2027 (the six months through July 2026), per the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"shares repurchased\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year 2025\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately $34 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share repurchases\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"shares repurchased\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"over $40.4 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share repurchases\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"shares repurchased\",\n     \"period\": \"first half of fiscal year 2027 (through July 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$39 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share repurchases\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 4,\n   \"ref\": \"S43\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07 (July window)\",\n   \"statement\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecast custom ASICs to grow at a 27% CAGR through 2033, compared with 16% for AI accelerators.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S44\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-02\",\n   \"statement\": \"On its August 2026 earnings call, Nvidia said it returned 60% of first-half fiscal 2027 free cash flow to shareholders, ahead of its 50% target, and that going forward it intends to increase the return of free cash flow net of strategic uses.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow returned to shareholders\",\n     \"period\": \"first half fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"60%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share of free cash flow returned via buybacks and dividends, company statement\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow return target\",\n     \"period\": \"first half fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"50%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company target for share of free cash flow returned to shareholders\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S45\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's sales grew 83% in the twelve months to fiscal Q2 2027, per Trefis.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales growth\",\n     \"period\": \"twelve months to fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"83%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"trailing twelve months\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S46\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's revenue and profit climbed in the triple digits in its latest three-month period, to $96 billion in revenue and $59 billion in profit, according to the article, continuing the double- and triple-digit quarterly growth it says Nvidia delivered in recent years.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"latest three-month period\",\n     \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"profit\",\n     \"period\": \"latest three-month period\",\n     \"value\": \"$59 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S47\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07 (July window)\",\n   \"statement\": \"Amazon disclosed that its Trainium business has reached multi-billion-dollar scale.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S48\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"In fiscal Q2 2027, NVIDIA's hyperscale revenues increased 117% year over year to $48.71 billion, while ACIE revenues, which include AI clouds, industrial and enterprise customers, jumped 138% to $40.31 billion.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Hyperscale revenues\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$48.71 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Up 117% year over year\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"ACIE revenues\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$40.31 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Includes AI clouds, industrial and enterprise customers; up 138% year over year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S49\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's revenue rose 96% year over year to $178 billion and net income surged 161% to $118 billion in the first half of its fiscal 2027, the period ended July 26, 2026, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$178 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company-wide revenue, up 96% year over year\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n     \"period\": \"First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$118 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company-wide net income, up 161% year over year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S50\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"statement\": \"The article states that Nvidia's data center business is still growing 117% year over year, without specifying which quarter the growth rate refers to.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"data center revenue growth, year over year\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"117%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company's data center business; quarter not specified\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S51\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"China H200 sales as share of data-center revenue\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"below 1%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia China H200 sales, share of data-center revenue\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S52\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-30\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia told investors in its prior-quarter report that it expects 70% revenue growth in its next fiscal year, citing massive AI demand, according to The Motley Fool.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"next fiscal year (FY2028, ending late January 2028)\",\n     \"value\": \"70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company revenue growth year over year, attributed by Nvidia to massive AI demand\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S53\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's Grace CPUs, launched in 2021, generated over $5 billion in revenue over the last year, the article reports.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Grace CPU revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"last year\",\n     \"value\": \"over $5 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Grace CPUs; trailing 12 months\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S54\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia expects $20 billion in CPU sales this year, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"CPU sales\",\n     \"period\": \"this year (calendar 2026 as described in the article)\",\n     \"value\": \"$20 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company expectation for stand-alone CPU sales\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S55\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in the last quarter, as reported by qz.com.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"last quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"75%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company gross margin, as reported\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 11,\n   \"ref\": \"S56\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-30\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin was 75% in fiscal second-quarter 2027 and is expected to be in the 72% to 73% range for fiscal 2028, with memory costs having risen more than management expected, as reported by Fool.com.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"75%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company gross margin\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"expected range; memory costs have risen more than management expected\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S57\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07 (July window)\",\n   \"statement\": \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, up more than 200% year over year.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S58\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA raised its dividend 25-fold in June and set a policy to return at least 50% of free cash flow to shareholders, according to its public June disclosures.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"dividend increase\",\n     \"period\": \"June 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"25-fold\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow returned to shareholders\",\n     \"period\": \"Policy set in June 2026; forward-looking\",\n     \"value\": \"at least 50% of free cash flow\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company capital-return policy per its public June disclosures\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S59\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days; management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Days of sales outstanding\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"60 days\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Average time to collect cash after a sale; management attributes the increase to extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers shipped over multiple quarters\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 4,\n   \"ref\": \"S60\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-30\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's operating margin stands at 65%, a rate the article says the company has roughly maintained for the last two years.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Operating margin\",\n     \"period\": \"Last two years (roughly maintained)\",\n     \"value\": \"65%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company operating margin; the article says the rate has been roughly maintained for the last two years\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S61\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"Zacks states that NVIDIA's net profit margin is 63.7%, compared with Micron's 55.9%, and that NVIDIA's return on equity of 96% exceeds Micron's 72.5%.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net profit margin\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"63.7%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA, compared with Micron's 55.9%\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S62\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-30\",\n   \"statement\": \"On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives said the company had begun shipping a small volume of H200 accelerators to China but that these shipments amounted to less than 1 percent of its data-center revenues.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"H200 accelerator shipments to China as a share of datacenter revenues\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2\",\n     \"value\": \"less than 1 percent\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"shipments of a small volume of H200 accelerators to the region\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S63\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-02\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's non-GAAP EPS was $2.22 in fiscal Q2 2027, up from $1.59 in fiscal Q4 2026, a gain of about 40% in two quarters; its adjusted EPS for the latest four reported quarters was around $6.95 versus $4.64 for fiscal 2026, up about 50%. Nvidia now treats stock-based compensation as a cost in adjusted earnings and restated earlier quarters to match, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Adjusted; stock-based compensation treated as a cost\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal Q4 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$1.59\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Adjusted; restated\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n     \"period\": \"Latest four reported quarters\",\n     \"value\": \"around $6.95\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Adjusted\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$4.64\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Adjusted\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS growth\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal Q4 2026 to fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"around 40%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Two quarters\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS growth\",\n     \"period\": \"Latest four reported quarters vs fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"about 50%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S64\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, a figure the article says far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue growth guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company guidance\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Consensus revenue growth estimate\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"45%\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Wall Street model previously expected, per the article\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 11,\n   \"ref\": \"S65\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-05\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Volume sales\",\n     \"period\": \"Next year\",\n     \"value\": \"double\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's own expectation, stated as underpinned by the Vera Rubin release\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S66\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-05\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA returned $26.0 billion to shareholders in the quarter and had $99.0 billion remaining under its buyback authorization.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Buyback authorization remaining\",\n     \"period\": \"As of the report\",\n     \"value\": \"$99.0 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"remaining under buyback authorization\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S67\"\n  }\n ],\n \"questions\": [\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"Q5\",\n   \"request\": \"Thesis v5 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 600,000,000,000 USD: below 600,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 750,000,000,000 USD: from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 750,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD: from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year\\\") / rung 4 from 850,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD: from 850,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 1,000,000,000,000 USD or more: 1,000,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 51 items]\\n[Not shown: 1 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e102 [party_statement; Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ad3e04be52c448172362b020 2026-09-23] Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, an estimate cited in a Fool.com opinion column on 23 September 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e163, e171, e241, e312, e323, e728, e1056; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e103 [third_party_view; Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor); original source Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor) 2026-09-23; from gnews:ad3e04be52c448172362b020 2026-09-23] The Fool.com opinion column states that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 2026, offered as the basis for continued growth in AI computing demand.\\n    Quote: Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e119, e162, e242, e311, e324, e367, e674, e781, e825, e973, e1037, e1055; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e105 [fact; barchart.com; original source Amazon.com (AMZN) ; from gnews:aa0d7cffe1e3c710b6347ad4 2026-09-23] The article cites a recent deal between Nvidia and Amazon (AMZN) involving 2 million GPUs, described as one reason NVDA remains a compelling long-term holding in 2026; no deal value or timing is given.\\n    Quote: The recent deal with Amazon (AMZN) involving 2 million GPUs and billions being spent on buybacks and dividend payments make NVDA an even more compelling long-term holding in 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e243, e768; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e107 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source S&P Global ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] S&P Global recently forecast that technology companies will spend an estimated $1.3 trillion on AI 'next year' — i.e. calendar 2027, as reported in a 23 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: S&P Global recently forecast that tech companies will spend an estimated $1.3 trillion on AI next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e305, e469; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e119 [third_party_view; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source 24/7 Wall St. ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article states hyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027.\\n    Quote: Hyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e162 [party_statement; The Motley Fool (Keithen Drury); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:915fe84b4c7f7b4decd1665d 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's most recent earnings call, the company said data center capital expenditures by the big five AI hyperscalers will approach $800 billion this year (2026) and are estimated to rise to $1.3 trillion next year (2027), as reported by The Motley Fool.\\n    Quote: During its most recent earnings call, Nvidia noted that this year's data center capital expenditures from the big five AI hyperscalers will approach $800 billion. Next year, that figure is estimated to rise to $1.3 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e186 [fact; Zacks (citing Marvell's Aug. 27 press release); original source Marvell Technology, Inc. 2026-08-27; from gnews:ab420e4b49c5ff4b8b3e5bdc 2026-09-24] Marvell Technology reported record fiscal second-quarter 2027 revenue of $2.739 billion, up 37% year over year, with Data Center revenue growing 46% year over year, according to its Aug. 27, 2026 press release.\\n    Quote: Revenues for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 reached a record $2.739 billion, up 37% year over year, according to the company's Aug. 27 press release. With Marvell supplying vital infrastructure for data center systems, its Data Center revenues grew even faster, up 46% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e321; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e187 [party_statement; Zacks (Marvell management guidance); original source Marvell Technology, Inc. 2026-08-27; from gnews:ab420e4b49c5ff4b8b3e5bdc 2026-09-24] Marvell Technology management guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $3.15 billion, plus or minus 5%, about 15% sequential growth at the midpoint, and remained optimistic about revenue growth through fiscal 2027 and 2028.\\n    Quote: For the fiscal third quarter of 2027, management expects revenues to reach $3.15 billion, plus or minus 5%. At the midpoint, it represents roughly 15% sequential growth. Management also remained optimistic about revenue growth throughout fiscal year 2027 and 2028.\\n- e242 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source The Motley Fool (Keithen Drury) 2026-09-24; from gnews:807e30726ee85ca7193b837f 2026-09-24] The article states that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year (calendar 2026), a third-party estimate of the combined data center capital spending that underpins Nvidia's AI chip demand.\\n    Quote: Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e264 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source Meta Platforms (partnership with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] Meta Platforms partnered with AMD on 6 gigawatts of capacity, using the same template as the OpenAI deal, with the first wave also due during the second half of 2026 and AMD issuing Meta a large warrant package.\\n    Quote: Meta Platforms used the same template. The social media giant partnered with AMD on 6 gigawatts, with the first wave also due during the second half of 2026, again with AMD issuing a large warrant package.\\n- e305 [rumour; Chris Neiger, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0acda86a3f25c028313f3af6 2026-09-25] Unnamed latest estimates cited by the article put global AI infrastructure spending at about $750 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\\n    Quote: And there's little sign that AI infrastructure spending is slowing. The latest estimates are for global AI infrastructure spending to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027 -- up from about $750 billion this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e311 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-09-25; from gnews:3eef853ce8ed9acd105923c4 2026-09-25] The article states, without naming a source, that the five largest AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in the current year (calendar 2026).\\n    Quote: The five largest AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year alone.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e312 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3eef853ce8ed9acd105923c4 2026-09-25] NVIDIA has projected that global data center capital expenditures will reach between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, a multiyear demand forecast the article cites as the basis for visibility into future demand.\\n    Quote: The company has projected that global data center capital expenditures will reach between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e102; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e324 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source Keithen Drury (fool.com), aggregating the big five AI hyperscalers 2026-09-26; from gnews:f72f4de1d44f4ee9244c7f89 2026-09-26] The fool.com author states that the big five AI hyperscalers are currently spending around $800 billion, presented against Nvidia's $3 trillion to $4 trillion 2030 global data center capex estimate.\\n    Quote: Right now, the big five AI hyperscalers are spending around $800 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e325 [fact; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:f72f4de1d44f4ee9244c7f89 2026-09-26] Alphabet is spending $200 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, which the fool.com author cites alongside Google Cloud's growth as evidence that AI infrastructure demand will stay elevated.\\n    Quote: During the second quarter, this division grew at a remarkable 82% pace, and with Alphabet spending $200 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, the growth rate will likely stay elevated for a long time.\\n- e358 [fact; James Brumley (fool.com); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:8962d069f4fb250cdc08e115 2026-09-26] Microsoft reported 18% year-over-year revenue growth for its quarter ended June 2026, led by 43% growth in its Azure cloud business where its AI revenue is recorded; author James Brumley notes both figures topped analysts' estimates. Microsoft is one of the large hyperscaler buyers of NVIDIA data-center hardware, so the reported growth bears on the demand backdrop for NVIDIA's products, though the item gives no NVIDIA figures.\\n    Quote: Based on the company's results for the three months ending in June, clearly not everyone agrees. Shares soared on its 18% year-over-year revenue growth, led by 43% growth of its Azure business, where its artificial intelligence numbers are recorded. Both numbers topped analysts' estimates, too.\\n- e359 [party_statement; James Brumley (fool.com); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:8962d069f4fb250cdc08e115 2026-09-26] Microsoft said it is looking for 45% revenue growth from its AI businesses for the quarter ending September 2026, and reported a backlog of $678 billion of contracted business already lined up and waiting to be booked as revenue, according to James Brumley's account of the company's results and outlook. As a large buyer of NVIDIA data-center hardware, the size of Microsoft's contracted AI backlog and expected AI revenue growth bear on the demand outlook for NVIDIA's products; the item gives no NVIDIA figures.\\n    Quote: The company's even looking for 45% revenue growth from its AI businesses for the quarter ending this month, with a backlog of $678 billion worth of business that's already lined up just waiting to be booked as revenue.\\n- e367 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] NVIDIA expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 2026, with that figure rising to $1.3 trillion in 2027 (\\\"next year\\\"), per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury citing the company's large industry outlook.\\n    Quote: As for a large industry outlook, Nvidia expects the big five AI hyper-scalers to spend nearly $800 billion in 2026, with that figure rising to $1.3 trillion next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e469 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source S&P Global Ratings ; from gnews:02b796f20d201c8380e0ae4d 2026-09-28] S&P Global Ratings said in August 2026 that combined hyperscaler capital expenditure is projected to exceed $1.3 trillion by 2027, as companies race to build AI infrastructure including data centers, CNBC reported.\\n    Quote: Combined hyperscaler capital expenditure is projected to exceed $1.3 trillion by 2027, S&P Global Ratings said in August, as companies race to build AI infrastructure including data centers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e674 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7968b6992410f494f08511b3 2026-09-30] The article reports, attributing the figures to Nvidia's projections for its key customers' capex, that the big five AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data-center build-outs in calendar 2026 ('this year'), with current estimates for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion in 2027 ('next year').\\n    Quote: The big five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data center build-outs this year, and the current estimates are for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion next year. That's just according to Nvidia's projections, which are likely pretty accurate, given what it knows about its key customers' plans.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e733 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang estimated that a one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion, or about $10 billion a year spread over six years, while the same capacity currently generates about $50 billion a year in rental revenue.\\n    Quote: A one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion. Spread over six years, that works out to about $10 billion a year. Yet that same capacity currently brings in about $50 billion a year in rental revenue, Huang said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e760 [rumour; Financial Times (citing unnamed people familiar with the matter), reported by Investing.com; original source Amazon 2026-10-01; from gnews:886f027fa46edb25b85723b2 2026-10-01] Amazon is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips to external investors, planning to spin thousands of the chips — currently used in data centers across the U.S. — into a special purpose vehicle that would raise debt from outside investors and lease the chips back to Amazon, with Amazon offering an equity stake of up to 10% in the vehicle and, per the report, holding no stake in the entity. The Financial Times reported the talks, citing people familiar with the matter, as aimed at strengthening Amazon's balance sheet through a more asset-light approach and as part of a broader push by U.S. hyperscalers toward less balance-sheet-heavy financing of their data center buildouts.\\n    Quote: Amazon is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia chips to external investors, the Financial Times reported on Thursday, citing people familiar with the matter. … Amazon held talks with investors in recent weeks to gauge interest in the deal, where the company plans to spin off thousands of Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle, the FT report said. … The e-commerce gi\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e838, e840, e891, e929, e960, e971, e1007, e1042, e1159; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e761 [party_statement; Investing.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:886f027fa46edb25b85723b2 2026-10-01] Amazon said it will spend over $200 billion in capital expenditure this year (calendar 2026), a bulk of which will go towards its Amazon Web Services cloud unit to buy more chips and build more data centers.\\n    Quote: The ecommerce giant said it will spend over $200 billion in capital expenditure this year, a bulk of which will go towards its Web Services cloud unit to buy more chips and build more data centers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e839, e892, e1044; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e781 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article states top-five hyperscaler capital expenditure is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Balancing that risk, top-five hyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e825 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:23646c683c86bed389e13357 2026-10-01] Nvidia's own estimates project the big hyperscalers' data center spending at nearly $800 billion this year (calendar 2026) and $1.3 trillion next year (calendar 2027), with the article noting those are Nvidia's numbers and that the hyperscalers may start sharing 2027 capital spending guidance when they report quarterly results.\\n    Quote: The growth case leans on the big hyperscalers, whose data center spending is projected at nearly $800 billion this year and $1.3 trillion next year, according to Nvidia's own estimates. … Hyperscalers may start sharing 2027 capital spending guidance when they report quarterly results.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e838 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance / finance.yahoo.com); original source Amazon ; from gnews:c5cace3648af0ab7a2100087 2026-10-02] Amazon is seeking external investors for a financing arrangement involving approximately $8 billion of advanced Nvidia chips, according to a Financial Times report citing people familiar with the matter: Amazon has held discussions with investors in recent weeks over a proposed special purpose vehicle that would hold thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips, lease them back to Amazon for use in US data centres, and raise debt from external investors, with investors offered an equity interest of up to 10% in the vehicle and Amazon retaining no equity stake itself. The item does not indicate that Amazon has reached a final agreement with investors or completed the transaction.\\n    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ:AMZN) is seeking external investors for a financing arrangement involving approximately $8 billion of advanced Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) chips, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … According to the report, Amazon has held discussions with investors in recent weeks to assess interest in a proposed structure that would place thousands of Nvidia Grace Blac\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e839 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance / finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:c5cace3648af0ab7a2100087 2026-10-02] Amazon expects its capital expenditure to exceed $200 billion this year (calendar 2026), with a substantial portion allocated to its Amazon Web Services cloud business for additional chips and data-centre construction.\\n    Quote: Amazon expects capital expenditure to exceed $200 billion this year, with a substantial portion allocated to its Amazon Web Services cloud business for additional chips and data centre construction.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e761; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e840 [rumour; Financial Times, as reported by Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon.com Inc 2026-10-01; from gnews:25920cb5dac45b4dddd3218d 2026-10-02] Amazon.com Inc is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips to outside investors and lease them back, the Financial Times reported on Thursday, citing people familiar with the matter. Under the proposed deal Amazon would spin off thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell AI data-center chips (used in data centers across the US) into a special-purpose vehicle, lease them back from it, and raise funding from outside investors through debt issuance; it plans to offer an equity stake of up to 10% in the vehicle and would hold no stake itself. Amazon held talks with investors in recent weeks to gauge interest. The report says the deal aims to strengthen Amazon's balance sheet by shifting expensive chip costs to investors under a more asset-light approach; it is unconfirmed and no completion or timing is given.\\n    Quote: Amazon.com Inc (NASDAQ:AMZN) is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA, XETRA:NVD) chips to external investors while leasing them back, the Financial Times reported Thursday. … The e-commerce giant held talks with investors in recent weeks to gauge interest in the proposed deal, the newspaper reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The proposed deal ai\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e890 [fact; MarketWatch, cited by qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon announced a roughly 15% price increase on its EC2 Capacity Blocks for ML service, which lets customers reserve access to Nvidia chips ranging from the older A100 to the newer B300; the updated rates take effect next week, according to MarketWatch.\\n    Quote: Amazon $AMZN announced a roughly 15% price increase on its EC2 Capacity Blocks for ML service, which lets customers reserve access to Nvidia $NVDA chips ranging from the older A100 to the newer B300, with updated rates taking effect next week, according to MarketWatch .\\n- e891 [rumour; Financial Times, cited by Reuters and qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon has approached outside investors about a deal to transfer roughly $8 billion in Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle and lease them back; the SPV would be funded through debt sold to outside investors, Amazon would hold an equity interest of as much as 10%, and the chips are already operating at more than a dozen sites across five U.S. states including Nevada and Virginia, according to the Financial Times.\\n    Quote: Separately, Amazon has approached outside investors about a deal that would transfer roughly $8 billion in Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle and then lease those chips back, according to the Financial Times, as cited by Reuters . Under the arrangement, the SPV would be funded through debt sold to outside investors, and Amazon would hold an equity interest of as much as 10\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e892 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon set its 2026 capital expenditure budget at $200 billion, up from $131 billion in 2025, with data centers, chips and related hardware accounting for the bulk of those outlays.\\n    Quote: Amazon 's capital expenditure budget for 2026 is set at $200 billion, up from $131 billion in 2025, with data centers, chips, and related hardware accounting for the bulk of those outlays.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e761; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e893 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon entered a $17.5 billion term loan in June and raised £4.25 billion through a debut sterling bond offering last week, extending a borrowing push that made it the largest bond issuer among major hyperscalers this year, with cumulative sales equivalent to nearly $100 billion.\\n    Quote: The company has been tapping a range of financing tools to fund that spending: it entered into a $17.5 billion term loan in June and raised £4.25 billion through a debut sterling bond offering last week, extending a borrowing push that has made it the largest bond issuer among major hyperscalers this year, with cumulative sales equivalent to nearly $100 billion.\\n- e926 [third_party_view; Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley, reported by TradingView/Stocktwits; original source Barclays (Tom O'Malley) 2026-10-01; from gnews:d91c54ec44da3c9a5cbef369 2026-10-02] Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley said on Thursday that Nvidia's hyperscaler revenue could run well above Barclays' current estimates: 'napkin math' based on Nvidia's latest IT spending data points to roughly $237 billion of hyperscaler revenue in 2026 and $401 billion in 2027, versus Barclays' existing estimates of $206 billion and $370 billion respectively. In an upside scenario he sees $246 billion in 2026 and $417 billion in 2027, and he noted the calculation may understate the opportunity because the capex comparison excludes SpaceX.\\n    Quote: On Thursday, Barclays analyst Tom O’Malley said Nvidia’s hyperscaler revenue could potentially run well above the firm’s current estimates, using “ napkin math ” based on Nvidia’s latest IT spending data. … His calculations point to roughly $237 billion in hyperscaler revenue in 2026 and $401 billion in 2027, compared with Barclays’ existing estimates of $206 billion and $370 billion, respectively\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e815; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e960 [rumour; fxleaders.com; original source Amazon (reported by the Financial Times from unnamed sources) ; from gnews:4d8fb36192b66cfb50e49f46 2026-10-02] Amazon is reportedly exploring a financing structure, reported by the Financial Times, that could move approximately $8 billion of Nvidia chips into a special-purpose vehicle funded by outside investors: Amazon has discussed transferring thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips used across more than a dozen U.S. data centers into the vehicle, which could issue debt and sell as much as a 10% equity stake to external investors, with Amazon continuing to use the chips after the transfer as a more asset-light way to finance part of its infrastructure build-out. Unconfirmed report; no completion, pricing or timing is disclosed.\\n    Quote: Amazon is reportedly exploring a financing structure that could move approximately $8 billion of Nvidia chips into a special-purpose vehicle funded by outside investors. … According to the Financial Times, Amazon has discussed transferring thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips across more than a dozen U.S. data centers into the vehicle. The structure could issue debt and sell as much as a 10% \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e971 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:7b31f6f72da28162f972b402 2026-10-03] Amazon has set up an $8 billion special purpose vehicle that buys Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips and leases them back to AWS clients. The article describes the structure as an off-balance-sheet financing tool for AI hardware intended to let AWS keep building AI capacity without carrying all the hardware on its own balance sheet as cloud capital demands rise. The item gives no date for the vehicle, its terms or its lease commitments.\\n    Quote: Amazon has set up an $8b special purpose vehicle to buy and lease back Nvidia (NasdaqGS:NVDA) Grace Blackwell chips to AWS clients. … The structure is designed as an off balance sheet financing tool for AI hardware as cloud capital demands rise. … Shifting thousands of Grace Blackwell chips into an external vehicle gives AWS a way to keep building AI capacity without stacking all the hardware on i\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e972 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:b7e22d5ba15d6483a30d2389 2026-10-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang reaffirmed his projection that data center capital expenditures will reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion annually by 2030, saying he believes the projection is still accurate.\\n    Quote: Jensen Huang, one of the co-founders of Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA), is no stranger to making bold predictions. One that he has been floating for a few years is that data center capital expenditures will reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion annually by 2030. That's a massive increase from today's levels, and he just commented that he believes that projection is still accurate.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1036; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e102 (); both are in force: Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, an estimate cited in a Fool.com opinion column on 23 September 2026.\\n- e973 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:b7e22d5ba15d6483a30d2389 2026-10-03] Nvidia expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026.\\n    Quote: In 2026, Nvidia expects the big five artificial intelligence (AI) hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1008 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon projects calendar-2026 capital expenditures of approximately $220 billion, about 10% above its previous $200 billion forecast, driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure centered on AWS.\\n    Quote: Amazon's projected capital expenditures for this year stand at approximately $220 billion, up about 10% from the previous forecast of $200 billion. The surge is driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure, centered on its cloud business, Amazon Web Services (AWS).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1160; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e761 (); both are in force: Amazon said it will spend over $200 billion in capital expenditure this year (calendar 2026), a bulk of which will go towards its Amazon Web Services cloud unit to buy more chips and build more data centers.\\n- e1009 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon's AWS backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\\n    Quote: AWS's backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\\n- e1010 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Andy Jassy, Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said in July 2026 that even with significantly increased investment Amazon would struggle to meet all AI computing demand, that it would not have enough capacity to satisfy all demand in 2026 and the same would be true in 2027, and that demand already secured for 2028 was extraordinary.\\n    Quote: Andy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, said in July that even with significantly increased investment, the company would struggle to meet all AI computing demand. … At the time, he stated: \\\"We won't have enough capacity to satisfy all the demand we have in 2026, and the same will be true in 2027.\\\" He added that \\\"the demand already secured for 2028 is extraordinary.\\\"\\n- e1037 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:97a074da716a2f2655c2885d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the article's account of the company's expectation.\\n    Quote: In 2026, Nvidia expects the big five artificial intelligence (AI) hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1043 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon spent $54.2 billion on capital projects in the second quarter, 68% more than a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Amazon spent $54.2 billion on capital projects in the second quarter, 68% more than a year earlier.\\n- e1044 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy committed to roughly $200 billion in spending for 2026.\\n    Quote: Andy Jassy committed to roughly $200 billion in spending for 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e761; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1045 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon's free cash flow over the past 12 months turned negative at -$7.6 billion, and its long-term debt rose to $119.1 billion from $65.6 billion a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Free cash flow over the past 12 months turned negative at -$7.6 billion. Long-term debt rose to $119.1 billion from $65.6 billion a year earlier.\\n- e1055 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:fdee99ebb64a9e468197951e 2026-10-04] NVIDIA management estimates the big five AI hyperscalers will spend about $800 billion on capital expenditures this year (2026), and that adding other players takes the total to around $1 trillion, according to this fool.com column.\\n    Quote: What's noteworthy about this forecast is that Nvidia management believes the big five AI hyperscalers will spend about $800 billion on capital expenditures this year. When other players are added in, that figure could reach around $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1158 [fact; Zacks Investment Research; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:28d4f9293b76613111469c90 2026-10-05] Zacks reported that Meta Platforms spent more than $100 billion on its AI investments in 2026, cited in the article as the source of Wall Street's concern about Meta's spending and its roughly 25x P/E; the item does not break the figure into data-center capital expenditure versus other AI investment such as talent.\\n    Quote: In 2026, Meta spent more than $100 billion on its AI investments. In fact, Meta even poached top AI talent from industry peers for a cool $100 million. The enormous spending has created fear, uncertainty, and doubt across Wall Street.\\n- e1159 [rumour; Financial Times (cited by TechRepublic); original source Amazon ; from gnews:7a1f9bd7788ce53c481edc8e 2026-10-05] Amazon is reportedly in talks with investors over a roughly $8 billion financing deal that would transfer thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell AI chips into a special-purpose vehicle and lease them back to Amazon for continued use in its data centers, according to the Financial Times.\\n    Quote: The company is in talks with investors over a roughly $8 billion financing deal that would place thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle and lease them back to Amazon, according to the Financial Times. … The proposed deal would allow Amazon to transfer thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle (SPV), then lease the hardware back for c\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1160 [party_statement; TechRepublic; original source Amazon ; from gnews:7a1f9bd7788ce53c481edc8e 2026-10-05] Amazon expects roughly $220 billion in capital expenditures in 2026, with spending focused heavily on AWS infrastructure, AI chips and data centers.\\n    Quote: The company expects roughly $220 billion in capital expenditures in 2026, with spending focused heavily on AWS infrastructure, AI chips and data centers .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1008; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1179 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry), a key builder and integrator of Nvidia-based AI servers, reported record September 2026 revenue of $36.5 billion (NT$1.16 trillion), up 25.7% from August and 38.4% from a year earlier — the first month above NT$1 trillion, exceeding the prior record of NT$946.5 billion set in July. Foxconn said its Cloud and Networking Products business grew significantly in September on \\\"strong pull-in momentum for AI products,\\\" with strong year-over-year growth in the segment.\\n    Quote: One of the largest assemblers of Apple (AAPL) products and key builder of Nvidia (NVDA)-based AI servers reported September revenue of $36.5 billion (NT$1.16 trillion) , up 25.7% from August and 38.4% from a year earlier. … This is the first time Foxconn's monthly revenue topped NT$1 trillion and surpassed the previous record of NT$946.5 billion set in July. … Foxconn said its Cloud and Networking\\n- e1180 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn reported third-quarter 2026 revenue of $95.3 billion, up 20.4% sequentially and 47.1% year over year, and nine-month 2026 revenue of $241.57 billion, up 39.5% from a year earlier, with both periods setting records; Foxconn builds and integrates Nvidia-based AI systems such as GB200 and GB300 NVL72 racks.\\n    Quote: The strength continued through the quarter. Foxconn reported third-quarter revenue of $95.3 billion, up 20.4% sequentially and 47.1% year over year. Nine-month revenue reached $241.57 billion, up 39.5% from a year earlier, with both periods setting records.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1098; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1181 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn said it expects AI-related operations to continue growing in the fourth quarter of 2026 alongside the traditional second-half peak season for ICT products, while cautioning that a volatile global political and economic environment remains a risk.\\n    Quote: Foxconn expects AI-related operations to continue growing in the fourth quarter, alongside the traditional second-half peak season for ICT products. The company cautioned that a volatile global political and economic environment remains a risk.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which is rung 3. Amazon's updated approximately $220 billion total-capex projection is about $20 billion above the previous $200 billion level; because the increase is attributed to data centers and AI infrastructure, it lifts the roughly $800 billion reference to an implied roughly $820 billion, not by the full $220 billion. Amazon's $54.2 billion Q2 capital-projects spend, up 68% year over year, is consistent with elevated spending but is not annualized flat to create a separate forecast. Meta's 6-GW partnership and the roughly $60 billion-per-GW, six-year data-center cost benchmark imply about $360 billion total, or $60 billion per year at full scale; however, only a first wave is due in H2 2026\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 3,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"Roughly $820 billion: start from the nearly $800 billion reference and add about $20 billion for Amazon's projection rising from $200 billion to approximately $220 billion. The other cited amounts do not establish an additional 2026 data-center-capex amount in hand.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still within rung 3. Meta's 6-GW partnership has a large theoretical full-scale cost, but only its first wave is due in H2 2026; it is not evidence that the full volume is in hand. Meta's reported AI investment total does not isolate data-center capex. Amazon's $8 billion SPV could affect how some investment is financed or reported, but the evidence does not establish a 2026 capex reduction; even an $8 billion adjustment would leave the estimate in rung 3. Supplier growth is supportive of demand but does not quantify a change to hyperscaler capex.\"\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"Q12\",\n   \"request\": \"Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\\\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\\n    Quote: GF Securities says Nvidia (NVDA) is scaling out co-packaged optics, or CPO, faster than expected, creating another potential growth driver for Lumentum. … The forecast is striking. GF Securities expects Nvidia's CPO switch shipments to reach 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, rising to 16,000 in early 2027 and 30,000 by mid-2027. For all of 2027, shipments could reach 100,000 units.\\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\\n    Quote: In Q4, the company generated $1.01 billion in revenue, more than doubling from a year ago and extending its streak of year-over-year growth to eight straight quarters. … The increase was widespread throughout the company. Components' revenue grew 103% to $649.4 million, while Systems' revenue increased 123% to $356.9 million. Profitability also improved, as the gross margin climbed to 50% and the \\n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\\n    Quote: Management anticipates the trend to persist through fiscal 2027 as well. Lumentum is projecting an increase of more than $150 million in sales for the first quarter, along with gains in profitability, as revenue for the period is expected to be in the neighborhood of $1.225 billion to $1.275 billion and adjusted earnings per share are projected to range between $4.05 and $4.35.\\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\\n    Quote: GF Securities sees Lumentum benefiting directly from that shift. The firm forecasts Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028. … Near-packaged optics, or NPO, could become an even larger opportunity. GF Securities estimates Lumentum's NPO laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The supplied market-facing reference is GF Securities’ forecast of 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in 2027, exactly the rung 1 boundary. Because this is a forecast for a product with no shipment volume shown in hand, it is discounted: the supplier evidence supports optical demand but does not establish Nvidia CPO switch unit volume or convert to 100,000 Nvidia switches. The most likely outcome is therefore below 100,000, while no evidence shows a formal abandonment or other decision against the event as a whole; rung 3 is the best forecast.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 3,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies calendar-2027 shipments below 100,000, but does not support a defensible more precise unit estimate: Lumentum's projected CPO/NPO laser revenues span products and are not attributable or convertible to Nvidia switch units. No evidence establishes formal abandonment, so the outcome remains open.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is GF Securities' estimate of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027, which sits at rung 1. The evidence supports an optical-demand ramp, but the 100,000 figure remains an analyst estimate rather than Nvidia shipment volume: Lumentum's revenue and guidance cover broader business, while its projected CPO and NPO laser revenues are not converted into Nvidia switch units. Discounting the unshipped product target, the most likely outcome remains below 100,000, but there is no evidence of formal abandonment or another decision against the event as a whole; therefore rung 3 is the forecast.\"\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"request\": \"Thesis v16 (custom-silicon, theme, focus \\\"Spending on custom AI silicon (ASICs) at cloud providers in calendar 2026\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\\nLadder: rung 1 Below $40 billion: Custom-silicon spending is less than $40 billion. / rung 2 $40 billion to below $60 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $40 billion and less than $60 billion. / rung 3 $60 billion to below $80 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $60 billion and less than $80 billion. / rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $80 billion and less than $100 billion. / rung 5 $100 billion or more: Custom-silicon spending is at least $100 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 2 items]\\n- e327 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\\n- e502 [party_statement; Fool.com analyst, reporting Marvell’s statement; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it has more than 50 potential custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, representing potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion; no timeframe beyond “in the long run” is specified.\\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v6 (open; last_end) What share of calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?: now rung 3 Neutral; reasons: The closest market reference is the estimate that NVIDIA’s AI-chip share will be 75% in 2026, which is rung 3; longer-term estimates of 70%–75% and near 75% point to the same rung, while undated 80% estimates are less specific. The implied outcome is about 75%: those estimates concern the broader AI-chip market rather than precisely calendar-2026 data-center accelerator spending, but they are the best quantified proxies supplied. NVIDIA’s strong training position and Huawei’s low performance-adjusted share support a higher outcome; the lower inference share, growing custom-silicon alternatives\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No supplied consensus, company guidance, or stated spending plan gives a market reference or a quantified 2026 spending estimate. The reported September manufacturing start is late in the year and gives no production volume or spending figure; it therefore does not imply that custom-ASIC spending reaches $40 billion. v6's neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom ASICs taking some share, but does not quantify custom-ASIC spending. The most likely outcome remains below $40 billion.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 1,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"The implied calendar-2026 spending remains below $40 billion. Marvell’s $75 billion is potential lifetime revenue across more than 50 opportunities with no specified timeframe, not 2026 spending; Meta’s reported September manufacturing start gives no production volume or spend figure, implying at most an unquantified late-year contribution. v6’s neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom silicon taking some share, but does not quantify its spending.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"No quantified market consensus, company guidance, or stated 2026 spending plan is supplied, so there is no reference rung to place on the ladder. The new evidence points to some 2026 custom-silicon activity, but neither item quantifies calendar-2026 provider spending; the implied outcome remains below $40 billion rather than crossing the $40 billion boundary.\"\n   },\n   \"ref\": \"Q13\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"Q1\",\n   \"request\": \"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 54 items]\\n[Not shown: 22 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e60, e274, e791, e1050, e1144; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1138; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) has already invested $1.5 billion and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the IPO.\\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e143, e1063; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136, e141, e778; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \\\"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e798; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e498 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, including potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies that would protect lenders if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to cover the debt, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the talks; the discussions are at an early stage and may not result in any deals.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held talks with insurance companies over ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as the chipmaker seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, the Financial Times reported on Tuesday. … The discussions include potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies, which could protect lenders if a borrower defaults and N\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e581, e599, e738; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e499 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, and is working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers, people familiar with the discussions told the Financial Times (Howden declined to comment); NVIDIA has also explored using insurance groups to syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, with the potential size of deals possibly exceeding the balance sheets of even large insurers, and has considered joining consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, while people familiar with the discussions said the company was also working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers. Howden declined to comment, the report said. … The potential structures could extend beyond traditional insurers. Nvidia has explored using insura\\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e582, e828, e992; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e581 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] NVIDIA has held early-stage discussions with insurance companies, working with insurance broker Howden Re, about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips, including potentially insuring loans made to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies so lenders are protected if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to repay the debt. The effort is part of CEO Jensen Huang's push to make NVIDIA chips and AI infrastructure easier for external investors to finance; NVIDIA provided at least one insurer with information on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing capacity, explored arrangements under which insurers could syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, and considered participating in financing consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers. The FT, citing people familiar with the matter, said the talks remain at an early stage and may not result in agreements; no pricing, counterparties or amount of financing that could be covered was disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held discussions with insurance companies about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips as the company seeks to expand demand for artificial intelligence infrastructure beyond major technology groups, the Financial Times reported. … The discussions include potentially insuring loans made to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies, accordi\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\\n    Quote: According to the supplied material, Nvidia has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e778 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e791 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9126c31bb44c2cc3f54c641a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company is using its own balance sheet to support purchases by some AI labs, that those AI labs should contribute roughly a quarter of its business the following year, and that some will call this support circular financing (NVIDIA helping fund customers who then buy from NVIDIA).\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is also using its own balance sheet to support purchases by some AI labs. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said those AI labs should contribute roughly a quarter of its business the following year. Management also said some people will call that support circular financing. The term means NVIDIA helps fund customers who then buy from NVIDIA.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e796 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA outlined more than US$500 billion of compute financing agreements and plans with partners including Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, intended to mobilize more than US$500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\\n    Quote: Nvidia outlined more than US$500b of compute financing agreements and plans with partners such as Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to mobilize over US$500b of third party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e830, e1011, e1049, e1129, e1137, e1164; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e798 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA is in talks to invest up to US$10 billion in Anthropic, and is pursuing further, unsized investments in AI firms including Perplexity and Decart AI, according to the article; no terms or completion are reported.\\n    Quote: is in talks to invest up to US$10b in Anthropic, and is pursuing further investments in AI firms including Perplexity and Decart AI\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e328; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e828 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Nvidia said independent financiers in its $500 billion infrastructure-financing push will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project, and that Nvidia may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities — a contingent credit backstop that would support lenders' recoveries on GPU-backed customer financing.\\n    Quote: Nvidia says independent financiers will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project. It may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e829 [third_party_view; Reuters, via gurufocus.com; original source Credit investors (cited by Reuters) ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Credit investors, as reported by Reuters, said banks commonly depreciate GPUs over three to four years, while Nvidia argues its leading systems can keep earning money much longer; the article says that gap in collateral values could mean more equity upfront, tighter lending terms or higher borrowing costs for GPU-backed financing.\\n    Quote: According to Reuters, credit investors said banks commonly depreciate GPUs over three to four years, while Nvidia argues its leading systems can keep earning money much longer. That gap matters. Lower collateral values could mean more equity upfront, tighter lending terms or higher borrowing costs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e994; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e830 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] The article states that Nvidia's $500 billion infrastructure-financing push is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\\n    Quote: The $500 billion is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e927 [fact; The Information, reported by TradingView/Stocktwits; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d91c54ec44da3c9a5cbef369 2026-10-02] Nvidia completed its final $10 billion investment in OpenAI's latest funding round, according to The Information, part of the strategic capital backing OpenAI and its plans to expand AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has also completed its final $10 billion investment, showcasing the size of the strategic capital backing OpenAI and its plans to expand AI infrastructure.\\n- e952 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia committed up to $100 billion to OpenAI in September 2025, but its final stake came to $30 billion, with another $10 billion going to Anthropic, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also funds its own customers. It committed up to $100 billion to OpenAI in September 2025, but the final stake came to $30 billion, with another $10 billion going to Anthropic.\\n- e961 [rumour; fxleaders.com; original source Unnamed lenders and credit investors (reported) ; from gnews:4d8fb36192b66cfb50e49f46 2026-10-02] Lenders reportedly question Nvidia's plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion, with banks and credit investors said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral and some lenders reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections. Unconfirmed, attributed to unnamed lenders; it does not establish that Nvidia has granted any guarantee or credit support.\\n    Quote: The financing discussion comes as lenders reportedly question Nvidia’s plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion. … Banks and credit investors are said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral. Some lenders are reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e988, e1017, e1034; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e988 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources and credit managers ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Unnamed banking sources and credit managers told Reuters that some lenders want stronger guarantees than Nvidia initially outlined for its chip-backed financing plan, even for Nvidia's AI chips, as they try to determine how long the chips can generate revenue. The sources also said bankers and asset managers doubt Nvidia's chips can serve as long-term collateral on their own and want greater protections around Nvidia's $500 billion financing plan.\\n    Quote: Some lenders want stronger guarantees than the company initially outlined, even for Nvidia’s industry-leading AI processing power, as they try to determine how long the revenue-generating life of its chips will last, banking sources and credit managers told Reuters. Several requested anonymity to discuss the financing structures. … Bankers and asset managers said they have doubts about whether Nvi\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e961; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e989 [fact; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August a $500 billion AI financing plan with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR that envisioned using Nvidia chips as collateral with limited guarantees to give AI developers access to Nvidia compute.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s plan, announced in August with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR, envisioned using its chips as collateral with limited guarantees, allowing AI developers to gain access to Nvidia’s compute. The strategy was seen as an expensive but potentially necessary way to create a financing market similar to the one used to finance aircraft.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e990 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia has said some deals under its $500 billion financing initiative could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has said some deals could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing that the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1130; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e961 (); both are in force: Lenders reportedly question Nvidia's plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion, with banks and credit investors said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral and some lenders reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections. Unconfirmed, attributed to unnamed lenders; it does not establish that Nvidia has granted any guarantee or credit support.\\n- e991 [rumour; Reuters; original source Three unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Three banking sources not part of Nvidia's original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its financing deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. The sources added that the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade asset comparable to aircraft.\\n    Quote: Three banking sources familiar with the matter but not part of the original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. … For now, the sources said, the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade ass\\n- e992 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Banking sources said Nvidia is providing previously unreported details to address lender concerns, and that tens of billions of dollars in loan deals in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections. Some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees, and the initial deals will be secured by Nvidia chips and backed by customer contracts and Nvidia's underlying guarantee, according to the sources.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is seeking to address some of these concerns, the sources said, providing details that had not previously been reported. … Tens of billions of dollars in loan deals currently in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections, one source said. A second source said some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees. … The initial deal\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e993 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang; Nvidia citing third-party studies) ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has argued Nvidia GPUs have a useful life of up to a decade, and Nvidia has cited third-party studies showing major cloud companies extending server depreciation periods to five or six years from three or four, plus a Barkr finding that GB300 NVL72 systems could have a useful life of nine to 10 years.\\n    Quote: Huang has addressed some of the skepticism by arguing that Nvidia’s graphics processing units, or GPUs, have a useful life of up to a decade. … Nvidia has pointed to several third-party studies showing that major cloud companies are extending the depreciation periods for servers to five or six years, from three or four. … The company has also cited a finding from Barkr, which provides valuations f\\n- e994 [third_party_view; Reuters; original source Tony Trzcinka (Impax Asset Management); Andrew Chang (S&P Global Ratings) ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Impax Asset Management's Tony Trzcinka said banks typically underwrite GPUs over a 3-4 year depreciation schedule, versus Nvidia's decade claim, and that investors are likely to demand higher interest rates, larger financial cushions and stronger repayment protections before backing chip-secured loans. S&P Global Ratings' Andrew Chang said S&P takes a conservative view of the chips' value.\\n    Quote: “Banks typically underwrite GPUs over a 3-4 year depreciation schedule,” Trzcinka of Impax said. … “That is different than Nvidia, which argues top-tier GPUs can earn revenue for a decade,” he said, adding that investors are likely to demand higher interest rates, larger financial cushions and stronger repayment protections before backing loans secured by AI chips. … “Nvidia would imply that the G\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e829; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e995 [fact; Reuters, citing S&P and Moody's; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy's Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody's.\\n    Quote: Nvidia itself previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy’s Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody’s.\\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1050 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA expects the AI labs it supports to account for roughly a quarter of its business next year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA expects the AI labs it supports to account for roughly a quarter of its business next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1063 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA disclosed in August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. The guarantees generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults; they cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases. The article describes this as contingent exposure rather than a current $105 billion cash bill.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA also disclosed August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. They generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults. The guarantees cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases; they are contingent exposure, not a current $105 \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1106 [fact; Benzinga; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia is backing AI infrastructure by providing credit support for 4.25 GW at SB Energy's Ohio campus and investing $1.5 billion in the developer, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is already backing that infrastructure, providing credit support for 4.25 GW at SB Energy's Ohio campus and investing $1.5 billion in the developer.\\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e990; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1136 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] On its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call on 26 August 2026, NVIDIA management said the company has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\\n    Quote: On its fiscal Q2 2027 call on August 26, 2026, management said NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1137 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] On the same fiscal Q2 2027 call (26 August 2026), NVIDIA announced partnerships with six infrastructure investors to set up financing platforms that are planned to raise over $500 billion of outside capital.\\n    Quote: It also announced partnerships with six infrastructure investors to set up financing platforms that will raise over $500 billion of outside capital.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1138 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA structures its dealings with NeoClouds (newer AI-focused cloud providers) by guaranteeing a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility's capacity and taking a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level, according to the article's description of the arrangement.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA goes further for NeoClouds, the newer cloud providers built for AI. It guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility’s capacity, and it takes a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1144 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said in August 2026 that it expects the AI labs it supports with its balance sheet to contribute roughly a quarter of its business in the following year, and acknowledged that some will call this circular financing.\\n    Quote: Management said in August that it expects the AI labs it supports with its balance sheet to contribute roughly a quarter of its business in the following year. It also acknowledged that some will call this circular financing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1146 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] The wait for NVIDIA to be paid rose to 60 days in fiscal Q2 2027 as the company gave certain investment-grade customers longer payment terms on large purchases; the article flags the fiscal Q3 2027 call, scheduled for 17 November 2026, as the next checkpoint on that figure.\\n    Quote: The fiscal Q3 2027 call is scheduled for November 17. One figure to follow then is how long NVIDIA waits to be paid. That wait rose to 60 days in Q2 as NVIDIA gave certain investment-grade customers longer payment terms on large purchases.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e989; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1164 [fact; The Motley Fool (Daniel Foelber); original source NVIDIA (with a consortium of six financial institutions) ; from gnews:b0cf215ea487392e67e586be 2026-10-05] The Motley Fool reported that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, an arrangement that enables customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly. The item does not state how much of the $500 billion Nvidia itself bears or whether Nvidia guarantees any of the financing.\\n    Quote: Demand is so massive that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, enabling customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1174 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA is funding some of its own customers through investments, having put nearly $50 billion into AI labs, according to Trefis.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is also funding some of its own customers: it has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified evidence is NVIDIA's reported $108.5bn cap on guarantee obligations, already above rung 5's $50bn threshold. The $105bn OpenAI-related guarantee is part of the existing exposure, not an amount to add to the $108.5bn cap; the $500bn financing plan is capital mobilization, not NVIDIA's guarantee amount, and its up-to-25% residual-value support applies only to selected opportunities. Reports of stronger protections in financing deals could add exposure, but do not size NVIDIA's share. The implied disclosed amount therefore remains at least $108.5bn, firmly in rung 5.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 5,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"Forecast disclosed exposure remains at least about $108.5bn, the reported cap on guarantee obligations. A separate financing-program scenario could add as much as $125bn if 25% support applied across the full $500bn target, but the target is over time and support is limited to selected opportunities, so the evidence does not justify assuming that full amount by the FY2027 report.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position: rung 3, Neutral ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified exposure remains NVIDIA's reported $108.5bn cap on guarantee obligations, already above rung 5's $50bn boundary. Morningstar's $125bn figure is a maximum scenario for the separate $500bn financing target if the full target received 25% support; the evidence says support applies only to selected opportunities and the target is over time, so that maximum is not a forecast to add in full to the $108.5bn. The disclosed 2 GW and 4.25 GW support, minimum-revenue guarantees, and financing backstops may add exposure, but are not sized sufficiently to change the rung.\"\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"Q10\",\n   \"request\": \"Thesis v11 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \\\"The change in average DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, per TrendForce/DRAMeXchange data.\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 0 percent: below 0 percent / rung 2 from 0 percent to below 10 percent: from 0 percent to below 10 percent / rung 3 from 10 percent to below 20 percent: from 10 percent to below 20 percent (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 percent to below 30 percent: from 20 percent to below 30 percent / rung 5 30 percent or more: 30 percent or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 61 items]\\n- e881 [party_statement; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr), quoting an unnamed Nvidia spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] An Nvidia spokesperson told Gizmodo that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, and said Nvidia has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model to focus production on the Shield TV Pro.\\n    Quote: “The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry. We’ve also stopped producing the standard Shield TV model as we focus on production for Shield TV Pro.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e883 [fact; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr); original source Nvidia add-in-card (AIC) makers, per Gizmodo's reported market prices ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] Nvidia's RTX 50-series GPUs sold by all major add-in-card makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than at launch, the article reports; a GeForce RTX 5090 that launched at $2,000 in 2025 now sells for well over $7,000, or close to $9,000 from some makers.\\n    Quote: The company’s RTX 50-series GPUs across all major AIC (add-in card) makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than they did at launch. A GeForce RTX 5090, which launched for $2,000 in 2025, now costs well over $7,000 or close to $9,000 by some makers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e353; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e238 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\\n    Quote: Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e288; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e293 [party_statement; Zacks; original source Micron Technology 2026-06-24; from gnews:90ebbd46135613dc0cb8830f 2026-09-25] Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, Micron expects revenues to rise to around $50 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026. The company also projects a healthy gross margin of approximately 86%, supported by strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\\n- e355 [fact; Engadget (Husain Parvez); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9c36b37c17bf3bafb47e561 2026-09-26] NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition desktop GPU is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. Engadget (Husain Parvez) reports that NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, and that the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes. The article frames the card as a professional/workstation product aimed at AI developers, engineers and 3D artists rather than gamers.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. … NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, although the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes.\\n- e368 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\\n    Quote: While Micron could sit around and enjoy the elevated prices, it's actively expanding its production footprint to provide clients with more memory chips. That production capacity won't be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\\n- e369 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron does not expect the \\\"tightness\\\" in the memory-chip market to alleviate until 2028, per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury; memory chips are described as a component NVIDIA's and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without.\\n    Quote: Micron also doesn't expect the \\\"tightness\\\" in the memory chip space to alleviate until 2028\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e987; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e533 [third_party_view; Jacqueline Thomas (IGN); original source IGN 2026-09-28; from gnews:240dc4dbbafffa84b05b213d 2026-09-28] IGN reported, in its own account accompanying a story about stolen Nvidia-branded trailers, that RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026 and that Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps during the year. The article gives no percentage, dollar figure or source for the memory or graphics-card price levels, and frames it as part of an ongoing RAM crisis pushing hardware prices higher.\\n    Quote: These thefts probably aren't helping with the cost of hardware. RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026, and Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps this year. … It's no secret that GPUs are worth their weight in gold right now, with the ongoing RAM crisis shooting prices through the stratosphere.\\n- e793 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:5f9ed396852a9b7ad63bc5f3 2026-10-01] Michael Burry noted that older Nvidia chip architectures such as the A100 and H100 have seen stabilised or rising rental rates in recent months, but attributed the trend to acute memory and power supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability, writing that 'a chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability.'\\n    Quote: While older chip architectures like the A100 and H100 have seen stabilized or rising rental rates in recent months, Burry points out that this trend stems from acute supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability. … \\\"A chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability,\\\" Burry writes.\\n- e841 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:0f88bce495310b4588d29700 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced that the Shield TV Pro streaming device will be priced at $299.99, a $100 increase from its original $199.99, and that it has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model, focusing production on the Pro variant instead. NVIDIA attributed the increase to substantially higher component costs, including DRAM, across the industry; pricing takes effect October 2 and the Pro's specifications are unchanged.\\n    Quote: Today, NVIDIA is announcing that the Shield TV Pro will now be priced at $299.99, a $100 US price bump versus its original $199.99 price. NVIDIA says that the cost of components, including DRAM, has increased substantially across the industry, and that is why the Shield device will now feature a higher price. … Additionally, NVIDIA has also announced that it will cease the production of the standa\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e881, e882, e921, e922, e943, e946, e969, e1021, e1023, e1026, e1132, e1133; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e842 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] NVIDIA is releasing a new 64 GB unified-memory configuration of its DGX Spark AI supercomputer at a $4,999 price point, keeping the rest of the 128 GB model's specifications, and the unit will be available from 23 October 2026 through its manufacturing partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI. According to the report, the 64 GB variant replaces the 128 GB DGX Spark at the $4,999 price point, with the 128 GB model moving to even higher prices because of persistent increases in memory and other component costs.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is launching a new version of its DGX Spark AI Supercomputer which packs half the memory, but is priced the same as the 128 GB model. … Due to persistent hikes across memory and other essential components, NVIDIA is unable to sustain those prices, and while the original 128 GB model will see even higher prices, NVIDIA is releasing a new variant of the DGX Spark that replaces the 128 GB mode\\n- e843 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\\n    Quote: Although the Spark launched at $3999, it went through a range of price bumps, with the most significant being the $4699 and $4999 prices, which were listed a few weeks back. … Due to persistent hikes across memory and other essential components, NVIDIA is unable to sustain those prices, and while the original 128 GB model will see even higher prices, NVIDIA is releasing a new variant of the DGX Sp\\n- e853 [party_statement; Insider Monkey (published via Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA Corporation (management) ; from gnews:a10cd465475cc83dc7c5e184 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\\n    Quote: While management's gross margin outlook remains elevated at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, maintaining these historic profitability levels requires navigating escalating high-bandwidth memory costs and intensifying research and development expenditures for next-generation systems.\\n- e896 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia introduced a cut-down DGX Spark with half the memory and storage of the existing model — a 64 GB GB10-based system versus the 128 GB version — to be sold exclusively through hardware partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI, expected to retail at around $4,999. The article presents the cheaper, lower-capacity configuration as a response to the memory shortage.\\n    Quote: In an effort to provide a more affordable AI system, Nvidia is introducing a cut down version of the DGX Spark with half the memory and storage. The GB10-based systems will be offered exclusively through hardware partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP, and MSI, and are expected to retail for around $4,999. … While the 64 GB version’s lower MSRP should make the systems more accessible am\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e899, e903, e985; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e897 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia raised the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark to $6,950, an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year; the article attributes the increase to skyrocketing memory prices. The article adds that the new 64 GB model's $4,999 price is still 25 percent above what the 128 GB version retailed for at launch.\\n    Quote: The less-powerful SKUs are necessary after Nvidia jacked the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark on Friday to $6,950 — an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year. As you might have already guessed, skyrocketing memory prices are to blame for the massive price adjustment. … That makes the 64 GB model considerably less than Nvidia’s top-spec DGX Spark and GB10 systems given the ongoing memo\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1025, e1189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e843 (); both are in force: The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\\n- e900 [third_party_view; Jeffrey Kampman, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-10-02; from gnews:d77fe74e308e447bc64c8895 2026-10-02] Tom's Hardware reported that skyrocketing RAM prices make a chip permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X a barrier to entry rather than an asset, that 128GB GB10 systems, when in stock, currently cost roughly $7,000 to $9,000 each — far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP — and that with memory and storage prices ever-shifting, the new 64GB GB10 systems' $4,999 starting price may not hold for long.\\n    Quote: And skyrocketing RAM prices mean that a chip that’s permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X is more of a barrier to entry than an asset. … Given the ever-shifting prices of memory and storage right now, they might not stay there for long. Assuming you can find a 128GB GB10 system in stock, you can expect to pay roughly $7000 to $9000 for one right now, far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP\\n- e903 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system, halving the unified memory of the original 128GB system, to be sold through Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI from 23 October 2026 at prices starting at $4,999. That starting price is $1,000 (25%) above the original 128GB Founders Edition's $3,999 MSRP and $300 above NVIDIA's current $4,699 Founders Edition price for the 128GB model, though the 64GB configuration is not directly equivalent because NVIDIA says it will be sold exclusively through manufacturer partners who set their own configurations and regional pricing.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has announced a new 64GB version of DGX Spark , cutting the unified memory capacity of the original system in half. The new configuration will be available from Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI starting October 23 , with prices starting at $4,999 . … NVIDIA’s 128GB Founders Edition previously carried a $3,999 MSRP , meaning the new 64GB systems start $1,000 higher, or 25% above the or\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e896; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e904 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA raised the 128GB Founders Edition MSRP to $4,699 in February 2026 , citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\\n- e917 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] SK Hynix, a supplier of high-bandwidth memory to NVIDIA, reported revenue of $71.5 billion for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2025, year-over-year growth of 46.8%, net income of $31.6 billion and a 44.2% net margin, which the article calls a significant improvement in profitability versus prior periods.\\n    Quote: In the fiscal year ended Dec. 31, 2025, revenue reached $71.5 billion, representing year-over-year growth of 46.8%. Net income for the period was $31.6 billion, yielding a net margin of 44.2%. This represents a significant improvement in profitability compared with prior periods.\\n- e921 [fact; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (citing WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the remaining Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429, before tax) — a $100 increase — according to the article, which cites WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement.\\n    Quote: One of our favorite streaming gadgets, the standard Nvidia Shield TV , is no more. … According to WCCFTech , the Pro is increasing in price by 50% from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429). … The list price is now $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax)\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e922 [party_statement; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (quoting Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] In its announcement discontinuing the base Shield TV and raising the Shield TV Pro price, Nvidia said the reason is that \\\"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\\\" — a company statement attributing the change to industry-wide component and memory cost inflation.\\n    Quote: The reason, and the reason for discontinuing the base model, is AI: As Nvidia says in its announcement, \\\"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\\\".\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e923 [third_party_view; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar; original source TechRadar (Carrie Marshall) ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] TechRadar's Carrie Marshall wrote that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \\\"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\\\" — a journalist forecast that the component/memory cost squeeze extends beyond 2026.\\n    Quote: This is likely to be the last Shield TV Pro model for some time, because all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least, and Nvidia can already reach gamers on multiple platforms with its GeForce Now streaming service, which is available on all the major desktop and mobile platforms as well as smart TVs from the likes of LG and Samsung .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e944; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e931 [fact; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron Technology reported quarterly earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already high expectations by 5.73%, and said the quarter delivered record profits plus triple-digit revenue and earnings growth, which Zacks attributes to soaring AI demand. Zacks reported the results on the Wednesday before its 2 October 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Wednesday, Micron , the AI memory leader, crushed Wall Street's earnings expectations on every metric. Once again, the quarter delivered record profits and triple-digit revenue and earnings growth. … Micron reported earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already lofty expectations by 5.73%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1083, e1163; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e932 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra (Micron CEO), reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron's guidance for its fiscal 2027 first quarter did not disappoint, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said fiscal 2027 will be even stronger than the record quarter just reported, as reported by Zacks on 2 October 2026.\\n    Quote: In addition to the blockbuster quarter, Micron's Q1 2027 guidance did not disappoint, with CEO Sanjay Mehrotra stating that fiscal 2027 will be even stronger.\\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \\\"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1027; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e108 (); both are in force: The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n- e943 [fact; tech.yahoo.com (citing WCCFTech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax), a $100 increase. The article attributes both moves to AI-driven component costs, quoting Nvidia's announcement: \\\"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\\\".\\n    Quote: The Shield TV Pro will stick around, but is now $100 more expensive … One of our favorite streaming gadgets, the standard Nvidia Shield TV , is no more. … According to WCCFTech , the Pro is increasing in price by 50% from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429). The reason, and the reason for discontinuing the base model, is AI: As Nvidia says in its announcement, \\\"the cost of components, including mem\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e944 [third_party_view; tech.yahoo.com; original source tech.yahoo.com ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] The tech.yahoo.com article's assessment is that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \\\"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\\\"; it cites Nvidia's GeForce Now streaming service, available on major desktop, mobile and smart-TV platforms including LG and Samsung TVs, as an alternative route to gamers. The forecast carries no figure or named source.\\n    Quote: This is likely to be the last Shield TV Pro model for some time, because all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least, and Nvidia can already reach gamers on multiple platforms with its GeForce Now streaming service, which is available on all the major desktop and mobile platforms as well as smart TVs from the likes of LG and Samsung .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e923; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e946 [fact; Craig Donaldson, Pocket-lint (citing Wccftech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bdf9837d06e742f9f03a27a8 2026-10-02] Nvidia announced it is discontinuing its standard Shield TV and, effective October 2, 2026, raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, from $200 to $299, attributing the increase to rising component costs amid an AI-driven global memory shortage.\\n    Quote: In a recent statement, Nvidia confirmed that it's discontinuing its standard Shield TV while simultaneously raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, taking the price from $200 to $300 (via Wccftech ). … Nvidia says the main reason for the price increase is the rising cost of components, driven by the ongoing global memory shortage caused by AI demand, which Nvidia has played a part in.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e968 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50% to $299.99, up from its $199.99 launch MSRP, with no hardware upgrade, and its own online store now lists the device at the new price. NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has bumped the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50%, now retailing for $299.99, up from its launch MSRP of $199. … NVIDIA's own online store now lists the device for $299.99, confirming the new price. … NVIDIA has attributed the price increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\\n- e970 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source Roku and Google ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] Roku raised prices on several of its streaming devices and Google raised the price of the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99, both citing higher memory and component costs, part of a broader trend of rising streaming-device prices.\\n    Quote: The price increase also aligns with a broader trend of rising streaming-device costs. Roku has raised prices on several devices, citing higher memory and component costs, and Google recently raised the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99 for the same reason.\\n- e987 [party_statement; tweaktown.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:947ae81b89f5813da44a3364 2026-10-03] Memory supplier Micron said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance and expects the memory crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Micron has said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance, and expects the crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e369; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e997 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the US retail price of its Shield TV Pro streaming device from $199.99 to $299.99, effective October 2, 2026, roughly 50% above the $199 launch price of the same Tegra X1+ hardware introduced in October 2019; the company attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory.\\n    Quote: Starting October 2, SHIELD Pro will be priced at $299. The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry. … The change took effect on October 2, with the Shield TV Pro moving from $199.99 to $299.99 at retail. … The change took effect on October 2, with the Shield TV Pro moving from $199.99 to $299.99 at retail.\\nThe price change also leaves the Pro as the re\\n- e998 [party_statement; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA stated that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, giving that as its reason for raising the Shield TV Pro's price; the claim is the company's characterisation of industry-wide input-cost inflation and carries no figure.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory, which the company says have increased substantially across the industry. … The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry.\\n- e1003 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com ; from gnews:284a01485767b622f994fe39 2026-10-03] The article states that the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch and that chip prices are skyrocketing as a result, which it gives as the reason Micron is currently producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor.\\n    Quote: Right now, Micron is producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor. That's because the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch, and chip prices are skyrocketing as a result.\\n- e1012 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-10-03; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] The article reports that rising memory prices for AI servers were the primary driver of Amazon's upward capital-expenditure revision.\\n    Quote: The primary driver of the upward revision is rising memory prices for AI servers.\\n- e1019 [fact; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Nvidia and partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI will, from 23 October 2026, deliver a new entry-level version of the DGX Spark mini-PC that keeps the GB10 Blackwell GPU but halves RAM and SSD: 64 GByte of LPDDR5X memory at US$5,000, versus the smallest 128-GByte version that reached the market a few weeks earlier for around US$4,000. The 128-GByte DGX Spark model now costs US$6,000, 50 percent more than a year ago. The item notes Nvidia does not this time name the tight memory market as the reason, though the author says it is still obvious, and that individual manufacturers will likely set their own prices, with US$5,000 a guideline.\\n    Quote: Starting October 23, Nvidia and its partners like Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP, and MSI will deliver a new version of the DGX Spark series of mini-PCs. It still uses the GB10 chip with the Blackwell architecture for its GPU and is designed for running AI models locally. The catch: Compared to the model introduced about a year ago, the RAM and SSD are halved, but the price is higher. … The first \\n- e1020 [third_party_view; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia (product and memory supply); price observation by heise.de ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Heise reports that Nvidia's fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte, is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than 17,000 Euros; the article notes that GDDR7 memory for RTX cards, like LPDDR5 for the DGX Spark, was apparently not covered by the long-term supplies Nvidia said at the beginning of 2026 it had secured.\\n    Quote: However, LPDDR5, like GDDR7 for RTX cards, was apparently not included. Even the fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte , is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than a staggering 17,000 Euros .\\n- e1022 [party_statement; An unnamed Nvidia spokesperson, quoted by Ars Technica (reported by Yahoo Tech); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d823b7f949cb0596bcd02e4b 2026-10-03] An NVIDIA spokesperson told Ars Technica that from October 2 the SHIELD Pro would be priced at $299, and stated that the cost of components, including memory, \\\"has increased substantially across the industry\\\" — a company claim about industry-wide input cost inflation used to explain the device's price increase.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson told Ars Technica , \\\"Starting October 2, SHIELD Pro will be priced at $299. The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry.\\\"\\n- e1024 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia said it will begin shipping a derivative of its DGX Spark personal AI workstation, with unified memory halved to 64GB, from 23 October 2026 at a recommended price starting at $4,999 — $1,000 above the $3,999 launch price of the 128GB model a year earlier; the product will not be sold directly by Nvidia but distributed exclusively through six partner manufacturers (Acer, Asus, Dell Technologies, Gigabyte, HP, MSI). Halving memory cuts the largest model that can run on a single device from 200 billion to 100 billion parameters, and the item says the memory reduction is widely interpreted as a choice to lower bill-of-materials costs.\\n    Quote: Nvidia will begin shipping a derivative model of its personal AI workstation, the DGX Spark, with unified memory cut in half to 64GB starting October 23. The recommended price starts at $4,999 (approximately 6.7 million won). That's a full $1,000 (approximately 1.3 million won) more than the $3,999 (approximately 5.4 million won) launch price of the 128GB model a year ago. … The new product will n\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1188; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1025 [fact; The Register (cited by finance.biggo.com); original source Nvidia 2026-10-02; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia raised the recommended price of its 128GB DGX Spark again on October 2, to $6,950 — a 74% increase from the model's launch price a year earlier — as reported by US tech outlet The Register and cited in the item.\\n    Quote: According to US tech outlet The Register, Nvidia raised the recommended price of the 128GB model again on October 2, to $6,950. That's a 74% increase from the launch price a year ago, and the 64GB model's $4,999 price point is still 25% higher than the 128GB model's original launch price.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e897; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e904 (); both are in force: NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\\n- e1026 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 increase — with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM; Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\\n    Quote: On the same day, Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 (approximately 130,000 won) increase. The adjustment comes with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM. Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \\\"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\\\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e934; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1028 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported that its data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier — a result the item ties to the memory shortage.\\n    Quote: Micron's data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier.\\n- e1030 [fact; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported fiscal Q4 results that exceeded earnings expectations, with a significant increase in data center revenues, and warned of a continuing memory chip shortage.\\n    Quote: Micron Technology MU exceeded Q4 earnings expectations with a significant increase in data center revenues, while warning of a continuing memory chip shortage.\\n- e1031 [party_statement; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology said it expects its growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies, following its strong fiscal Q4 earnings.\\n    Quote: Following strong earnings, Micron Technology (MU) expects growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies.\\n- e1074 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia (CFO) ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia's CFO flagged 'extreme pricing conditions in memory' as a margin headwind.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \\\"extreme pricing conditions in memory\\\" as a margin headwind.\\n- e1083 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Micron Technology 2026-09-30; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after the Wednesday closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion, above Benzinga Pro's analyst estimate of $50.75 billion, and adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, above the $31.45-per-share estimate.\\n    Quote: Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after Wednesday’s closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion. Revenue surpassed Benzinga Pro’s analyst estimate of $50.75 billion. The semiconductor company also reported adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, exceeding the $31.45-per-share estimate.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e931; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e293 (2026-06-24); both are in force: Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\\n- e1107 [third_party_view; Benzinga; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley said that delayed data-center deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley said delayed deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\\n- e1108 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed.\\n    Quote: Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed\\n- e1123 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said it expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2027 because memory costs are rising.\\n    Quote: Margins are expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter because memory costs are rising.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e853 (); both are in force: NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\\n- e1132 [fact; The Verge (citing Ars Technica); original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:f674aa00a3b8657dbc4bb737 2026-10-05] NVIDIA raised the price of its Shield TV Pro streaming box by $100, from $199.99 to $299.99, starting on October 2nd, attributing the increase to higher component costs amid the industry-wide memory shortage, according to Ars Technica. The article frames the move as the memory shortage passing through into NVIDIA's own consumer hardware pricing.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s Shield TV streaming boxes are the latest victims of the memory shortage that has seen prices increase dramatically for everything from digital cameras to game consoles . When the Shield TV Pro launched nearly seven years ago with an upgraded Tegra X1+ processor, 3GB of RAM, and 16GB of storage, it sold for $199.99. But starting on October 2nd Nvidia increased its price by $100 to $299.99 \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1150 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source Micron Technology (with unnamed forecasters) ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\\n    Quote: Current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1190; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1151 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com (article); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:4cf776a64aef8ef7dde73e49 2026-10-05] Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June 2026 that DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply, and that he expects tight memory-market conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June that \\\"DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply\\\" and that he expects \\\"tight conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e1150 (); both are in force: MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\\n- e1163 [fact; Zacks Equity Research (Zacks Analyst Blog); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:4813fe2f3bcaef0438d6271c 2026-10-05] Micron Technology reported (in a release the item refers to only as dated 'Yesterday', in an article published 5 October 2026) fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue that surged 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, with EPS of $33.42 topping the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%; Zacks attributed the results to strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Yesterday, the company reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue surge of 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, while EPS of $33.42 topped the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%. The results reflect strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e931; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1169 [fact; fool.com (Bram Berkowitz); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:af32e2b77f410d23029454d7 2026-10-05] Memory maker Micron Technology, a supplier of DRAM and NAND used with GPUs, recently reported quarterly earnings that again beat analyst estimates and guided its current quarter above expectations, according to fool.com, in a year the article describes as supply-constrained for memory.\\n    Quote: Micron recently reported earnings that once again blew past analyst estimates, and it also provided guidance for the current quarter, which came in higher than expected.\\n- e1173 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA guided gross margin down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 74% expected in fiscal Q3 2027, and said it expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027, blaming memory prices that rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA does not expect its gross margin to hold. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and management expects 74% for Q3. Management then expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in Q4, and it blamed the price of memory, which rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1188 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia is launching a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a starting price of $4,999 when it goes on sale on 23 October 2026 — $1,000 more than the original 128GB model's $3,999 launch price, even though it offers half as much memory. The lower-memory unit keeps the same GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip and AI software stack and supports models of up to 100 billion parameters, versus up to 200 billion for the 128GB system.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA ) is launching a lower-memory version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a higher price than the original model cost at launch, underscoring how persistent memory shortages are pushing up the cost of AI hardware. … The new 64GB DGX Spark will start at $4,999 when it goes on sale October 23. … That is $1,000 more than the original 128GB models $3,999 launch price , despite offering\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1024; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1189 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source  ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] The original 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950, roughly 75% above its debut price, and partner versions have reportedly been selling for $5,999 to $9,000 depending on storage and configuration, according to the report cited by the article (which uses 'reportedly').\\n    Quote: The pricing contrast becomes even more striking when looking at the original model today. … According to the report, the 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950 , roughly 75% above its debut price . Partner versions have reportedly been selling anywhere from $5,999 to $9,000 , depending on storage and configuration.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e897; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1190 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Memory supplier Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028, with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance, as cited by the article.\\n    Quote: Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028 , with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1150; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1191 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia has attributed the rising prices of its systems to industry-wide memory supply constraints, which the article says may not ease soon.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has already blamed rising prices on industry-wide memory supply constraints, and those pressures may not ease soon.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The most likely outcome remains 30% or more: Micron’s latest results exceeded its prior revenue guide, customer commitments rose, and management says the DRAM imbalance persists with no line of sight to balance and demand exceeding supply in 2027 and 2028. Those facts support sustained contract-price strength, while capacity additions described as not coming online until mid-2027 or later offer little relief within calendar 2026. They do not translate directly into an average contract-price percentage, so the 30%+ forecast is a directional inference, not a reported or precisely calculable figure; devi\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 5,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"At least 30% (rung 5); the evidence supports a 30%+ forecast through persistent, multi-year DRAM supply tightness and reported extreme memory pricing, but does not permit a reliable point estimate for the calendar-year average contract-price change.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028, while more than 75% of its 2027 output is committed. Micron's strong results and explicit reports of extreme memory pricing reinforce the direction. Together these make a 30%+ annual average contract-price increase the most likely rung, though the evidence does not provide a direct contract-price series or a precise percentage. Consumer-product price rises and memory-cost comments are supporting context, not measurements of DRAM/HBM contract prices; the deployment-delay risk is a counterweight.\"\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"Q2\",\n   \"request\": \"Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 29 items]\\n[Not shown: 8 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips. … But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware. … On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 202\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e108 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source Micron Technology (as reported) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n    Quote: For example, Micron Technology 's memory chips are having a significant problem, with demand far outpacing supply. That's led to skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron, and global supply constraints.\\n- e113 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e526, e683, e766, e777, e1048, e1062, e1073; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\\n    Quote: At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. … Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e271 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, as summarised by Trefis from the latest earnings call.\\n    Quote: Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e272 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\\n    Quote: Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e557, e682, e866, e1123, e1173; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e49 (); both are in force: Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter, as reported by qz.com; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\\n- e393 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler (Fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e0b54b2bb227716c646f29f 2026-09-27] According to Fool.com columnist Geoffrey Seiler, Nvidia has locked in $500 billion worth of supply-constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the memory used to optimize its AI chips. The item gives no counterparty, contract term or date for the commitment.\\n    Quote: It locked in $500 billion worth of supply constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), which is needed to optimize AI chips\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1040; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e523 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Nvidia, Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek (as reported) ; from gnews:896d25da7b9b8340a736b2c0 2026-09-28] Nvidia, together with Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek, is reportedly raising its 2-nanometer orders at TSMC by 10% to 20%, according to the GuruFocus article of 28 September 2026, with the article tying the increase to customers preparing next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ), Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek are reportedly lifting 2nm orders by 10% to 20%. That gives TSMC additional visibility as customers prepare next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\\n- e619 [party_statement; taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-09-28; from gnews:0e3eeae7aedc281011283fa0 2026-09-29] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking to reporters at The Korea Society's annual gala in New York, said NVIDIA sits at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build-out in human history and will generate increasing cash, while continuing to make strategic and operational investments and having to secure supply-chain commitments.\\n    Quote: “We’re really fortunate that NVIDIA is at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build out in human history. We’re going to generate a lot of cash in the future. Now of course, we’ll continue to make strategic investments and operational investments and we have to secure supply chain commitments and so on and so forth. … But even then, with all of that, we’re going to generate more an\\n- e672 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's latest August 2026 earnings call, CEO Jensen Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, above analyst forecasts for 45% growth; he said the sales gain would be larger—in excess of 100%—if not for memory chip shortages.\\n    Quote: On its latest August earnings call, Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028. This was above analyst forecasts for 45% growth. The sales gain would be larger — in excess of 100% — if not for memory chip shortages , Huang said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\\n    Quote: Unconstrained demand growth, meaning what customers would buy if supply were unlimited, is running above 100%. In other words, Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\\n- e731 [rumour; Jensen Huang; original source Unnamed reports cited by Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang cited reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes were up 27% from one month to the next.\\n    Quote: Huang pointed to reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes up 27% from one month to the next.\\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n- e766 [fact; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of fiscal Q2 2027, largely tied to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: At the same time, NVIDIA is committing significant resources to secure supply. Supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of the second quarter, largely tied to memory procurement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e771 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70% and described the expectation as supply-constrained, indicating the outlook is limited partly by available supply rather than customer demand.\\n    Quote: Management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70%. It also described this expectation as supply-constrained, suggesting that the company's current revenue outlook is based partly on available supply rather than a lack of customer demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e777 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e807 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e35845cd1fb0497077e2d464 2026-10-01] NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States together with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron and Corning, according to the article; no timeline or contractual commitment amounts are given.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the U.S. with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron, Corning, and others.\\n- e873 [third_party_view; Dave Kovaleski, fool.com; original source fool.com (Dave Kovaleski) 2026-10-02; from gnews:edb22ee9082181453e2be651 2026-10-02] In this 2 October 2026 column, Fool.com's Dave Kovaleski forecasts that Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), Nvidia's main foundry, will generate some $33 billion of revenue from its largest customer, Nvidia, in calendar 2026. This is the author's forecast of Nvidia's annual payments to TSMC, not a reported actual or a disclosed contractual commitment.\\n    Quote: TSMC makes millions of advanced chips every year and will generate some $33 billion from its largest customer, Nvidia, in 2026.\\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \\\"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1027; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e108 (); both are in force: The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: Its own books carry weight too. Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, with $279 billion of that tied mostly to memory procurement.\\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \\\"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\\\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e934; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\\n    Quote: It is also the main HBM supplier to Nvidia, and the two companies recently formed an important supply and co-development partnership. As part of the deal, SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e393; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\\n    Quote: Its second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. … The filing allocated $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward. NVIDIA also said it may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orde\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1073 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \\\"extreme pricing conditions in memory\\\" as a margin headwind.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The implied amount is well above rung 5's $125bn floor, so it remains strongly bearish on this ladder. The reported $279bn is a current supply/capacity commitment balance, not the FY2027 year-end amount; the larger multi-year HBM estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 5,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"About $187bn as a disclosed-schedule run-off benchmark: $279bn of Q2 commitments less the $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. New commitments and cancellations could move the year-end balance, but the evidence does not quantify those changes; the likely outcome remains above $125bn.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (neutral); no consensus or company forecast for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The evidence instead points well above rung 5's $125bn floor: NVIDIA reported $279bn of supply and capacity commitments at FY2027 Q2, including $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. A simple run-off benchmark is therefore about $187bn ($279bn less $92bn), before any new commitments or cancellations. Ongoing supply constraints and strong guided growth make replenishment plausible, but do not quantify it; the separate $500bn multi-year memory estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified. Even the disclosed run-off benchmark remains in rung 5.\"\n   }\n  }\n ]\n}\n# 判断的尺子，第 1 版（D3 立场金标）\n\n写于 2026-11-02。Alex 的话：\"你已经改了好几次尺子了，到底什么是对的，以及你怎么 freeze。\"\n\n## 0. 一句话\n\n一道题判得对不对，拿它和**一位资深对冲基金分析师读完同样 14 天材料后的看法**比：他认为市场原来预期什么、材料推出来的结果是多少、\n相对预期是高是低；系统最后一次判断落的那一档，有没有把这个结果包进去。分析师不是我，不是写代码的人，不看系统的输出；他的看法写成文件、算出\nsha256、记进决定，之后一个字不改。\n\n## 1. 什么是\"对\"\n\n- **单位**：一道金标题 × 一个时点（14 天窗口末，2026-07-14 收盘后）。23 道题：22 道种子（`poc/seeds/nvda-2026-07-01-v2.json` v1–v22，每次运行里\n  编号固定）加金标 must_open 里种子没覆盖的 1 道（俄亥俄租约担保是/否，2026-07-01 的 must_open）。其余 27 条 must_open 都是这 23 道的同一赌注\n  （信用支持金额 ×10、Kyber 出货 ×11、云厂商资本开支 ×5、内存价格 ×1、中国放行 H200 ×2），映射写在 `questions-v1.json` 里，Alex 签。\n- **材料**：签过的抽取标准对这 14 天的读法，即金标 golden-v5（dev，118 篇）的 309 条事项（核心 86、辅助 151、可选 72），带日期、标题、\n  来源层、说话人、原文和意思。不用系统的账本（账本随每臂变，尺子不能随臂变），不用原始全文（那是重做抽取）。这和抽取的尺子是同一把：标准第 5 节\n  说的\"分析员会记的\"就是这些。\n- **分析师写什么**（每道题一条，`reader-brief.md` 是他拿到的全部说明，里面只有标准和 PRD 06 的原话，没有系统的提示词）：\n  1. `reference`：到 2026-07-14 为止市场预期这道题的答案是什么（一致预期、公司指引、既定计划或截止日），出自哪几条事项；材料里没有就写\n     \"没有，参照 = 现状\"。\n  2. `implied_outcome`：材料推出来的结果——数字题给一个数或一个区间，事件题给是/否和日期——怎么推的，出自哪几条。\n  3. `direction`：结果相对参照：above / in_line / below；事件题 yes / no。\n  4. `confidence`：high / medium / low（材料把这件事定到什么程度）。\n  5. `decisive_items`：撑起这个看法的 2–6 条事项编号；`against`：最强的反面一条。\n  6. `no_view`：材料里没有任何关于这道题的东西时为 true；这道题不进分数，记\"没练到\"。\n  7. `reasoning`：三到六句。只用材料，不用材料外的知识，每个说法都要有编号。\n- **分析师是谁**：两位互相独立、都没写过这套代码也没看过系统输出的模型读者（A：Codex；B：一个干净上下文的 Claude 读者），各自从同一个包\n  写一遍。都标明\"模型写的\"。**Alex 定（2026-11-02，D262）：金标 = 读者 B 的 23 条答案，原样；读者 A 的答案留作对照记录**，两者的差别\n  （区间或是/否不同 3/23：q09、q12、q23；方向标签不同 12/20）就是这把尺子自身噪声的记录（`reconcile-v1.md`）。\n- **判\"对\"的方法**（`score_judgement.py`，全部是代码）：取一次运行里每道题在 2026-07-14 及之前最后一次判断。数字题：金标的区间\n  [low, high] 和系统所选那一档的 [lo, hi) 有没有交集，有 = 一致；事件题：是/否相同 = 一致。没判过的题单独数（那是证据没到，D2 的事，不算\n  判断错）。另外打印两条零假设：**查表基线**（把金标写的参照数落进哪一档，就选那一档）和**永远中性基线**；判断要在这两条线之上才算有判断力。\n  读者之间的分歧率一起打印，那是尺子自身的噪声。\n- **第二层，读不是数**：分数之外，对一个样本的判断，由没写代码的读者看系统写的理由（从 judging-v6 起还有 reference 和 implied_outcome）\n  是不是从所示证据得出。这是 AGENTS §7.2 的\"分数是线索、trace 是测量\"。\n\n## 2. 怎么 freeze\n\n原则先于机制（D261，Alex 2026-11-02：\"我们做完的 review 过的尺子金标不能再动了\"）：这一页、读者说明、题单和金标在 Alex 审过签字之后一个字不改；之前只在建的过程中改，签字前不拿它打任何分。下面是把这句话变成代码和文件的做法。\n\n1. 读者只看 `packet-v1.json` 和 `reader-brief.md`，看不到任何运行、账本、提示词；读者的原始答案 `reader-A.json`、`reader-B.json` 原样保存，不改。\n2. 我不写金标。我做的只有：拼包（代码）、按 direction 机械对齐两位读者、把分歧按材料写成对照记录（引编号）；分歧由材料定，定不了的列给 Alex。\n3. 金标文件 `gold-v1.json` 带 `ruler_version: 1`、两位读者的 sha256、每道题的来源（A/B 一致，或裁定及理由）；文件的 sha256 写进\n   `gold-v1.sha256` 和决定 D261。Alex 签的是：23 道题的清单和映射、裁定过的题。签之前每个分数都标\"金标第 1 版，未签\"。\n4. `score_judgement.py` 开头读金标的 sha256 和 `ruler_version`，和自己写死的对不上就拒绝打分，打印两边的 sha；每份分数都印金标版本。\n5. 冻住后不改文件。发现错了，写进 `corrections-v1.json` 一条带日期的记录：哪道题、改前、改后、为什么；打分器不悄悄应用它，只在分数旁印\n   \"有 N 条更正未并入\"。更正积累到要并入时，出第 2 版：新文件、新 sha、决定里记，旧分数作废并说明。\n6. 这一页的定义本身改了（单位、材料、字段、判对的方法），就是第 2 版尺子：金标必须重写，打分器拒绝版本不符的金标。改定义只能由记录在 Alex\n   原话上的决定触发（AGENTS §7.1）。\n7. 一把尺子：判断这一步不再有别的评分标准。我昨天定的判断规则 D260 是系统这边的规则，不是尺子；读者的说明里没有它。\n\n**冻结记录**：2026-11-02，`gold-v1.json` sha256 `636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2`，写在 `gold-v1.sha256` 和 `score_judgement.py` 里；`corrections-v1.json` 为空。当天下午提过的两条改法（读者分叉时打三档分；五五开的题不计分）Alex 没采纳：一题一个区间，每题都算。\n\n这把尺子作为整体（抽取标准 + 题目 + 判断）写在 AGENTS §7.1 \"The frozen ruler\"，每个文件的 sha256 由 `tests/test_frozen_rulers.py` 守着：任何一个动了，整套测试变红。\n\n## 2′. 这把尺子沿用了什么，新的只有哪一层\n\n沿用、不重做：抽取标准页（第 3 版签字 D243 + D250 的四条界线）和 golden-v5 是这把尺子的**材料**，按 sha 引用（`packet-v1.json` 记了金标的 sha）；golden-v5 的题目部分（28 条 must_open，题目标准第 3 版）映射进 23 道题；种子 v2（D253）给题的身份和档的边界；PRD 06 的判断规则和 AGENTS §8 的分析师原话是读者说明里的全部规则；桌面尺子 README 的 D1、D2 不动。2026-10-27 的步骤 3/4 金标（`research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/`，24 例，量的是重复 / 替换 / 并入的决定，不是档位）里写的分析师标准 A（谁先说的，复述只加一个出处不加事实）、B（期间和定义要对上，季度不是全年）和这份说明一致，两者量的是不同步骤；那份金标是在旧账本上写的，助手自己写的，当时就标了不是独立金标。新的只有一层：判断这一步的档位从来没有金标，这是第一份，还有打分器。\n\n## 3. 这把尺子不量什么\n\n该开的题开了没有（D1）、证据挂得对不对（D2）、14 天里每天的路径、依赖和主题推力。它只量：窗口末，系统的档位有没有包住分析师认为的结果。\n\n## 4. 已知的弱点，写明\n\n- 读者是模型，不是人。金标是一位读者（B）的看法，Alex 选的；另一位（A）留作对照，两者在 3/23 道题上区间不交，这是尺子的噪声下限；Alex 的裁定是人的那一票。\n- 材料外的知识：事后结果（FY2027 年报、2026 年底 Rubin 出不出货）都在模型知识截止之后，无法作弊；但模型可能用它记得的\"常识\"补材料，说明里\n  禁止并要求每句引编号，对照时查。\n- 参照：材料里不一定有每道题的一致预期（内存价格、CoWoS 产能），那时参照是现状，direction 的意义弱一点，分数仍按区间交集算。\n- 种子的档不是围着一致预期写的（总收入题一致预期 $392B 落在中性档 350–400 的上沿）：所以分数不比\"看涨/看跌\"的标签，只比区间有没有包住。\n\n## 5. 文件\n\n`judgement-gold/`：`ruler-v1.md`（本页）、`reader-brief.md`、`build_packet.py` → `packet-v1.json` + `questions-v1.json`、\n`run_reader_codex.py` → `reader-A.json`、读者 B 的答案 `reader-B.json`、`reconcile.py` → `gold-v1.json` + `reconcile-v1.md`、\n`gold-v1.sha256`、`corrections-v1.json`、`score_judgement.py`。\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json\\\")); print(\\\"STATEMENTS\\\"); [print(x[\\\"ref\\\"],x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"statement\\\"]) for x in p[\\\"statements\\\"]]; print(\\\"QUESTIONS\\\"); [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",q.keys(),\\\"\\\\n\\\",str({k:v for k,v in q.items() if k \"'!= \"request\"})[:3000],\"'\"\\\\nREQUEST HEAD\\\",q[\\\"request\\\"][:1800]) for q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json\\\")); print(\\\"STATEMENTS\\\"); [print(x[\\\"ref\\\"],x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"statement\\\"]) for x in p[\\\"statements\\\"]]; print(\\\"QUESTIONS\\\"); [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",q.keys(),\\\"\\\\n\\\",str({k:v for k,v in q.items() if k \"'!= \"request\"})[:3000],\"'\"\\\\nREQUEST HEAD\\\",q[\\\"request\\\"][:1800]) for q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"STATEMENTS\nS1 2026-10-04 NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion of second-quarter revenue and $63.73 billion of operating income, an operating margin of about 66%, according to the article.\nS2 2026-09-22 NVIDIA's latest reported quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in total revenue, so roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI, according to GuruFocus's recounting of the results in its 22 September 2026 article.\nS3 2026-09-24 NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\nS4 2026-09-28 NVIDIA said on Monday 28 September 2026 that it authorized an additional $150 billion for its share repurchase program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion, which it described as the largest increase in a share repurchase authorization in history; the company said it expects to complete the total remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\nS5 2026-09-29 NVIDIA has a $150 billion share buyback plan, described by Yahoo Finance as the largest ever, which the article cites alongside its cash generation as justification for the repurchase programme.\nS6 2026-09-24 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue growth of 85% year over year, with Data Center revenues up 92% year over year, as retold by Zacks Equity Research in its Analyst Blog highlights.\nS7 2026-09-25 NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\nS8 2026-09-27 NVIDIA says its $108 billion revenue forecast for the current quarter assumes no China data-center chip revenue; any China data-center chip sales would come on top of that outlook.\nS9 2026-09-23 NVIDIA's second-quarter net income rose 126% year-on-year to $59.7bn, according to the Yahoo Finance UK article, which notes earnings grew roughly 4.5 times faster than the share price over the same comparison.\nS10 2026-10-01 NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 EPS of $2.22, topping the $2.09 consensus and marking its fifth consecutive beat, according to the article.\nS11 2026-10-04 Nvidia reported free cash flow of $27.02 billion in the fiscal year ended January 2024, $60.85 billion in fiscal 2025 and $96.68 billion in fiscal 2026.\nS12 2026-09-29 NVIDIA's free cash flow fell by more than half in the quarter before the 29 September 2026 article, mostly because accounts receivable rose by more than $22 billion; the article states the sales were booked but the cash had not yet been collected.\nS13 2026-09-25 Fool.com reports that Nvidia's sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively, in fiscal 2026, and that two customers make up more than a third of sales — a customer concentration it flags as a risk.\nS14 2026-09-23 Nvidia, in its quarterly results reported in the month before this 23 September 2026 Barchart article, posted what the article calls a market-beating fiscal Q2 and issued guidance for about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\nS15 2026-10-02 NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\nS16 2026-10-01 NVIDIA generated $24.08 billion in operating cash flow and $21.34 billion in free cash flow during fiscal Q2 2027.\nS17 2026-09-22 NVIDIA management guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, which the article says implies 89% growth.\nS18 2026-09-23 Nvidia told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the Morningstar author calculates at 2,400%. The author estimates roughly $25 billion per year would be returned to shareholders through the dividend, which would make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US market; that annual figure is the author's approximation, not a company-disclosed total.\nS19 2026-07 (July window) Broadcom AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion in Q2, up 143% year over year.\nS20 2026-09-29 Nvidia reported free cash flow of $69.9 billion for the first half of fiscal 2027, 77% higher than a year before, or around $35 billion a quarter; CEO Jensen Huang linked the buyback increase to the company's \"cash generation\" in the announcement.\nS21 2026-09-28 Former Wall Street analyst David Bennett, writing on X.com and quoted by the article, stated that Nvidia generated $74.4 billion of operating cash flow in the first half of fiscal year 2027 alone while still returning $46.1 billion to shareholders, which he said lets the company fund AI expansion and buy back stock at the same time.\nS22 2026-09-22 NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027.\nS23 2026-09-25 NVIDIA's board approved raising the quarterly dividend from $0.01 to $0.25 per share in May 2026, a 2,400% increase that lifted the quarterly payout from about $243 million (fiscal 2027 first quarter, when the penny dividend was still in force) to approximately $6.0 billion. The first $0.25 payment went out on 26 June 2026 to holders of record as of 4 June, and a second $0.25 distribution went ex-dividend on 10 September 2026 with payment scheduled for 1 October 2026; the new annual rate is $1 per share.\nS24 2026-10-05 NVIDIA spent $20 billion on share repurchases in its most recent quarter and $40 billion in the first half of fiscal 2027, split evenly at $20 billion in each of the first and second quarters, according to the article.\nS25 2026-09-29 Nvidia's diluted share count for its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026, was only about 1% smaller than a year before despite almost $40 billion of buybacks in the fiscal year's first half; earnings per share more than doubled.\nS26 2026-09-28 NVIDIA said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin.\nS27  NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\nS28 2026-09-29 In NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027, sales to the largest cloud companies were $49 billion, up 13% from the prior quarter, while sales to all other data center customers were $40 billion, up 25% from the prior quarter.\nS29 2026-09-28 Trefis reports that NVIDIA's operating margin — the share of revenue left after running costs — is 65%, the highest in ten years, versus a three-year average of 59% (the article does not state the exact measurement period).\nS30 2026-09-27 NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028, and management expects the higher prices to lift gross margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.\nS31 2026-09-27 The low end of NVIDIA management's gross margin guidance for fiscal Q3 2027 is 73.5%, per the article.\nS32 2026-09-22 fool.com reports that NVIDIA generated $192.9 billion in net income over the trailing 12 months, compared with $6.4 billion for AMD, citing this in a valuation comparison of the two AI chip makers.\nS33 2026-09-25 Fool.com reports that Nvidia's fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, produced revenue of nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, net income of approximately $120.1 billion, and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the previous fiscal year.\nS34 2026-10-02 In its latest reported quarter, Nvidia spent $19.7 billion on share buybacks and $6 billion on dividends, versus Apple's $25.9 billion of buybacks and $4 billion of dividends in Apple's fiscal 2026 third quarter; a year earlier, in the second quarter of Nvidia's fiscal 2026, Nvidia's buybacks and dividends combined totaled $10 billion.\nS35 2026-09-27 NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\nS36 2026-07 (July window) Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI from late 2026 into 2027.\nS37 2026-10-01 NVIDIA returned approximately $25.78 billion through share repurchases and dividends in fiscal Q2 2027, and in late September 2026 increased its share repurchase authorization by $150 billion, leaving a total of $235 billion available for future buybacks.\nS38 2026-09-26 Nvidia estimates that its revenue growth for fiscal 2027 will be around 70%, according to Fool.com contributor Keithen Drury.\nS39 2026-09-29 Nvidia management said demand for Vera Rubin currently implies its CPU revenue will more than double in fiscal 2028, which would position the company as one of the world's leading server CPU suppliers, according to the article.\nS40 2026-09-23 Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration, per the Morningstar article.\nS41 2026-09-24 NVIDIA management said Vera Rubin, its newest platform, is expected to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and put NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin versus about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\nS42 2026-10-05 NVIDIA's data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up from $39 billion in the quarter of the lost H20 sales.\nS43 2026-09-28 Nvidia repurchased approximately $34 billion of its shares in fiscal year 2025, over $40.4 billion in fiscal year 2026, and $39 billion in the first half of fiscal year 2027 (the six months through July 2026), per the article.\nS44 2026-07 (July window) Bloomberg Intelligence forecast custom ASICs to grow at a 27% CAGR through 2033, compared with 16% for AI accelerators.\nS45 2026-10-02 On its August 2026 earnings call, Nvidia said it returned 60% of first-half fiscal 2027 free cash flow to shareholders, ahead of its 50% target, and that going forward it intends to increase the return of free cash flow net of strategic uses.\nS46 2026-09-29 NVIDIA's sales grew 83% in the twelve months to fiscal Q2 2027, per Trefis.\nS47 2026-10-01 Nvidia's revenue and profit climbed in the triple digits in its latest three-month period, to $96 billion in revenue and $59 billion in profit, according to the article, continuing the double- and triple-digit quarterly growth it says Nvidia delivered in recent years.\nS48 2026-07 (July window) Amazon disclosed that its Trainium business has reached multi-billion-dollar scale.\nS49 2026-10-01 In fiscal Q2 2027, NVIDIA's hyperscale revenues increased 117% year over year to $48.71 billion, while ACIE revenues, which include AI clouds, industrial and enterprise customers, jumped 138% to $40.31 billion.\nS50 2026-09-25 Nvidia's revenue rose 96% year over year to $178 billion and net income surged 161% to $118 billion in the first half of its fiscal 2027, the period ended July 26, 2026, according to the article.\nS51 2026-09-26 The article states that Nvidia's data center business is still growing 117% year over year, without specifying which quarter the growth rate refers to.\nS52 2026-09-27 Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\nS53 2026-09-30 Nvidia told investors in its prior-quarter report that it expects 70% revenue growth in its next fiscal year, citing massive AI demand, according to The Motley Fool.\nS54 2026-09-29 Nvidia's Grace CPUs, launched in 2021, generated over $5 billion in revenue over the last year, the article reports.\nS55 2026-09-29 Nvidia expects $20 billion in CPU sales this year, according to the article.\nS56 2026-09-22 NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in the last quarter, as reported by qz.com.\nS57 2026-09-30 NVIDIA's gross margin was 75% in fiscal second-quarter 2027 and is expected to be in the 72% to 73% range for fiscal 2028, with memory costs having risen more than management expected, as reported by Fool.com.\nS58 2026-07 (July window) Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, up more than 200% year over year.\nS59 2026-09-24 NVIDIA raised its dividend 25-fold in June and set a policy to return at least 50% of free cash flow to shareholders, according to its public June disclosures.\nS60 2026-09-22 NVIDIA's days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days; management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\nS61 2026-09-30 NVIDIA's operating margin stands at 65%, a rate the article says the company has roughly maintained for the last two years.\nS62 2026-09-25 Zacks states that NVIDIA's net profit margin is 63.7%, compared with Micron's 55.9%, and that NVIDIA's return on equity of 96% exceeds Micron's 72.5%.\nS63 2026-09-30 On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives said the company had begun shipping a small volume of H200 accelerators to China but that these shipments amounted to less than 1 percent of its data-center revenues.\nS64 2026-10-02 Nvidia's non-GAAP EPS was $2.22 in fiscal Q2 2027, up from $1.59 in fiscal Q4 2026, a gain of about 40% in two quarters; its adjusted EPS for the latest four reported quarters was around $6.95 versus $4.64 for fiscal 2026, up about 50%. Nvidia now treats stock-based compensation as a cost in adjusted earnings and restated earlier quarters to match, according to the article.\nS65 2026-09-22 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, a figure the article says far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled.\nS66 2026-10-05 NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\nS67 2026-10-05 NVIDIA returned $26.0 billion to shareholders in the quarter and had $99.0 billion remaining under its buyback authorization.\nQUESTIONS\n\n dict_keys(['ref', 'request', 'answer']) \n {'ref': 'Q5', 'answer': {'rung': 3, 'implied_outcome': \"Roughly $820 billion: start from the nearly $800 billion reference and add about $20 billion for Amazon's projection rising from $200 billion to approximately $220 billion. The other cited amounts do not establish an additional 2026 data-center-capex amount in hand.\", 'reasons': \"The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still within rung 3. Meta's 6-GW partnership has a large theoretical full-scale cost, but only its first wave is due in H2 2026; it is not evidence that the full volume is in hand. Meta's reported AI investment total does not isolate data-center capex. Amazon's $8 billion SPV could affect how some investment is financed or reported, but the evidence does not establish a 2026 capex reduction; even an $8 billion adjustment would leave the estimate in rung 3. Supplier growth is supportive of demand but does not quantify a change to hyperscaler capex.\"}} \nREQUEST HEAD Thesis v5 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?\nLadder: rung 1 below 600,000,000,000 USD: below 600,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 750,000,000,000 USD: from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 750,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD: from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \"the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year\") / rung 4 from 850,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD: from 850,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 1,000,000,000,000 USD or more: 1,000,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 51 items]\n[Not shown: 1 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e102 [party_statement; Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ad3e04be52c448172362b020 2026-09-23] Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, an estimate cited in a Fool.com opinion column on 23 September 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e163, e171, e241, e312, e323, e728, e1056; the argument counts one claim, one mo\n\n dict_keys(['ref', 'request', 'answer']) \n {'ref': 'Q12', 'answer': {'rung': 3, 'implied_outcome': \"The evidence implies calendar-2027 shipments below 100,000, but does not support a defensible more precise unit estimate: Lumentum's projected CPO/NPO laser revenues span products and are not attributable or convertible to Nvidia switch units. No evidence establishes formal abandonment, so the outcome remains open.\", 'reasons': \"The reference is GF Securities' estimate of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027, which sits at rung 1. The evidence supports an optical-demand ramp, but the 100,000 figure remains an analyst estimate rather than Nvidia shipment volume: Lumentum's revenue and guidance cover broader business, while its projected CPO and NPO laser revenues are not converted into Nvidia switch units. Discounting the unshipped product target, the most likely outcome remains below 100,000, but there is no evidence of formal abandonment or another decision against the event as a whole; therefore rung 3 is the forecast.\"}} \nREQUEST HEAD Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\n    Quote: GF Securities says Nvidia (NVDA) is scaling out co-packaged optics, or CPO, faster than expected, creating another potential growth driver for Lumentum. … The forecast is s\n\n dict_keys(['request', 'answer', 'ref']) \n {'answer': {'rung': 1, 'implied_outcome': 'The implied calendar-2026 spending remains below $40 billion. Marvell’s $75 billion is potential lifetime revenue across more than 50 opportunities with no specified timeframe, not 2026 spending; Meta’s reported September manufacturing start gives no production volume or spend figure, implying at most an unquantified late-year contribution. v6’s neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom silicon taking some share, but does not quantify its spending.', 'reasons': 'No quantified market consensus, company guidance, or stated 2026 spending plan is supplied, so there is no reference rung to place on the ladder. The new evidence points to some 2026 custom-silicon activity, but neither item quantifies calendar-2026 provider spending; the implied outcome remains below $40 billion rather than crossing the $40 billion boundary.'}, 'ref': 'Q13'} \nREQUEST HEAD Thesis v16 (custom-silicon, theme, focus \"Spending on custom AI silicon (ASICs) at cloud providers in calendar 2026\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $40 billion: Custom-silicon spending is less than $40 billion. / rung 2 $40 billion to below $60 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $40 billion and less than $60 billion. / rung 3 $60 billion to below $80 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $60 billion and less than $80 billion. / rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $80 billion and less than $100 billion. / rung 5 $100 billion or more: Custom-silicon spending is at least $100 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 2 items]\n- e327 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\n- e502 [party_statement; Fool.com analyst, reporting Marvell’s statement; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it has more than 50 potential custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, representing potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion; no timeframe beyond “in the long run” is specified.\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v6 (open; last_end) What share of calendar-2026 dat\n\n dict_keys(['ref', 'request', 'answer']) \n {'ref': 'Q1', 'answer': {'rung': 5, 'implied_outcome': 'Forecast disclosed exposure remains at least about $108.5bn, the reported cap on guarantee obligations. A separate financing-program scenario could add as much as $125bn if 25% support applied across the full $500bn target, but the target is over time and support is limited to selected opportunities, so the evidence does not justify assuming that full amount by the FY2027 report.', 'reasons': \"The reference is the opening no-reference position: rung 3, Neutral ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified exposure remains NVIDIA's reported $108.5bn cap on guarantee obligations, already above rung 5's $50bn boundary. Morningstar's $125bn figure is a maximum scenario for the separate $500bn financing target if the full target received 25% support; the evidence says support applies only to selected opportunities and the target is over time, so that maximum is not a forecast to add in full to the $108.5bn. The disclosed 2 GW and 4.25 GW support, minimum-revenue guarantees, and financing backstops may add exposure, but are not sized sufficiently to change the rung.\"}} \nREQUEST HEAD Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 54 items]\n[Not shown: 22 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same cl\n\n dict_keys(['ref', 'request', 'answer']) \n {'ref': 'Q10', 'answer': {'rung': 5, 'implied_outcome': 'At least 30% (rung 5); the evidence supports a 30%+ forecast through persistent, multi-year DRAM supply tightness and reported extreme memory pricing, but does not permit a reliable point estimate for the calendar-year average contract-price change.', 'reasons': \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028, while more than 75% of its 2027 output is committed. Micron's strong results and explicit reports of extreme memory pricing reinforce the direction. Together these make a 30%+ annual average contract-price increase the most likely rung, though the evidence does not provide a direct contract-price series or a precise percentage. Consumer-product price rises and memory-cost comments are supporting context, not measurements of DRAM/HBM contract prices; the deployment-delay risk is a counterweight.\"}} \nREQUEST HEAD Thesis v11 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \"The change in average DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, per TrendForce/DRAMeXchange data.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?\nLadder: rung 1 below 0 percent: below 0 percent / rung 2 from 0 percent to below 10 percent: from 0 percent to below 10 percent / rung 3 from 10 percent to below 20 percent: from 10 percent to below 20 percent (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 percent to below 30 percent: from 20 percent to below 30 percent / rung 5 30 percent or more: 30 percent or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 61 items]\n- e881 [party_statement; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr), quoting an unnamed Nvidia spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] An Nvidia spokesperson told Gizmodo that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, and said Nvidia has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model to focus production on the Shield TV Pro.\n    Quote: “The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry. We’ve also stopped producing the standard Shield TV model as we focus on production for Shield TV Pro.”\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e883 [fact; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr); original source Nvidia add-in-card (AIC) makers, per Gizmodo's reported market prices ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] Nvidia's RTX 50-series GPUs sold by all major add-in-card makers now cost hundred\n\n dict_keys(['ref', 'request', 'answer']) \n {'ref': 'Q2', 'answer': {'rung': 5, 'implied_outcome': 'About $187bn as a disclosed-schedule run-off benchmark: $279bn of Q2 commitments less the $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. New commitments and cancellations could move the year-end balance, but the evidence does not quantify those changes; the likely outcome remains above $125bn.', 'reasons': \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (neutral); no consensus or company forecast for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The evidence instead points well above rung 5's $125bn floor: NVIDIA reported $279bn of supply and capacity commitments at FY2027 Q2, including $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. A simple run-off benchmark is therefore about $187bn ($279bn less $92bn), before any new commitments or cancellations. Ongoing supply constraints and strong guided growth make replenishment plausible, but do not quantify it; the separate $500bn multi-year memory estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified. Even the disclosed run-off benchmark remains in rung 5.\"}} \nREQUEST HEAD Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 29 items]\n[Not shown: 8 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVID\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json\\\"));\nfor q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]:\n print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"]); s=q[\\\"request\\\"]; print(\\\"DATES\\\", sorted(set(re.findall(r\\\"judged 2026-\\\\d\\\\d-\\\\d\\\\d\\\",s))));\n for line in s.splitlines():\n  if re.search(r\\\"\\\\b(2026|revenue|margin|Broadcom|Trainium|demand|cash flow|commitment|memory|repurchase|customer|Rubin|ASIC)\\\\b\\\",line,re.I) and line.startswith(\\\"- e\\\"): print(line[:1300])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json\\\"));\nfor q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]:\n print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"]); s=q[\\\"request\\\"]; print(\\\"DATES\\\", sorted(set(re.findall(r\\\"judged 2026-\\\\d\\\\d-\\\\d\\\\d\\\",s))));\n for line in s.splitlines():\n  if re.search(r\\\"\\\\b(2026|revenue|margin|Broadcom|Trainium|demand|cash flow|commitment|memory|repurchase|customer|Rubin|ASIC)\\\\b\\\",line,re.I) and line.startswith(\\\"- e\\\"): print(line[:1300])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### Q5\nDATES []\n- e102 [party_statement; Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ad3e04be52c448172362b020 2026-09-23] Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, an estimate cited in a Fool.com opinion column on 23 September 2026.\n- e103 [third_party_view; Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor); original source Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor) 2026-09-23; from gnews:ad3e04be52c448172362b020 2026-09-23] The Fool.com opinion column states that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 2026, offered as the basis for continued growth in AI computing demand.\n- e105 [fact; barchart.com; original source Amazon.com (AMZN) ; from gnews:aa0d7cffe1e3c710b6347ad4 2026-09-23] The article cites a recent deal between Nvidia and Amazon (AMZN) involving 2 million GPUs, described as one reason NVDA remains a compelling long-term holding in 2026; no deal value or timing is given.\n- e107 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source S&P Global ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] S&P Global recently forecast that technology companies will spend an estimated $1.3 trillion on AI 'next year' — i.e. calendar 2027, as reported in a 23 September 2026 article.\n- e119 [third_party_view; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source 24/7 Wall St. ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article states hyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e162 [party_statement; The Motley Fool (Keithen Drury); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:915fe84b4c7f7b4decd1665d 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's most recent earnings call, the company said data center capital expenditures by the big five AI hyperscalers will approach $800 billion this year (2026) and are estimated to rise to $1.3 trillion next year (2027), as reported by The Motley Fool.\n- e186 [fact; Zacks (citing Marvell's Aug. 27 press release); original source Marvell Technology, Inc. 2026-08-27; from gnews:ab420e4b49c5ff4b8b3e5bdc 2026-09-24] Marvell Technology reported record fiscal second-quarter 2027 revenue of $2.739 billion, up 37% year over year, with Data Center revenue growing 46% year over year, according to its Aug. 27, 2026 press release.\n- e187 [party_statement; Zacks (Marvell management guidance); original source Marvell Technology, Inc. 2026-08-27; from gnews:ab420e4b49c5ff4b8b3e5bdc 2026-09-24] Marvell Technology management guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $3.15 billion, plus or minus 5%, about 15% sequential growth at the midpoint, and remained optimistic about revenue growth through fiscal 2027 and 2028.\n- e242 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source The Motley Fool (Keithen Drury) 2026-09-24; from gnews:807e30726ee85ca7193b837f 2026-09-24] The article states that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year (calendar 2026), a third-party estimate of the combined data center capital spending that underpins Nvidia's AI chip demand.\n- e264 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source Meta Platforms (partnership with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] Meta Platforms partnered with AMD on 6 gigawatts of capacity, using the same template as the OpenAI deal, with the first wave also due during the second half of 2026 and AMD issuing Meta a large warrant package.\n- e305 [rumour; Chris Neiger, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0acda86a3f25c028313f3af6 2026-09-25] Unnamed latest estimates cited by the article put global AI infrastructure spending at about $750 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e311 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-09-25; from gnews:3eef853ce8ed9acd105923c4 2026-09-25] The article states, without naming a source, that the five largest AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in the current year (calendar 2026).\n- e312 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3eef853ce8ed9acd105923c4 2026-09-25] NVIDIA has projected that global data center capital expenditures will reach between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, a multiyear demand forecast the article cites as the basis for visibility into future demand.\n- e324 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source Keithen Drury (fool.com), aggregating the big five AI hyperscalers 2026-09-26; from gnews:f72f4de1d44f4ee9244c7f89 2026-09-26] The fool.com author states that the big five AI hyperscalers are currently spending around $800 billion, presented against Nvidia's $3 trillion to $4 trillion 2030 global data center capex estimate.\n- e325 [fact; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:f72f4de1d44f4ee9244c7f89 2026-09-26] Alphabet is spending $200 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, which the fool.com author cites alongside Google Cloud's growth as evidence that AI infrastructure demand will stay elevated.\n- e358 [fact; James Brumley (fool.com); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:8962d069f4fb250cdc08e115 2026-09-26] Microsoft reported 18% year-over-year revenue growth for its quarter ended June 2026, led by 43% growth in its Azure cloud business where its AI revenue is recorded; author James Brumley notes both figures topped analysts' estimates. Microsoft is one of the large hyperscaler buyers of NVIDIA data-center hardware, so the reported growth bears on the demand backdrop for NVIDIA's products, though the item gives no NVIDIA figures.\n- e359 [party_statement; James Brumley (fool.com); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:8962d069f4fb250cdc08e115 2026-09-26] Microsoft said it is looking for 45% revenue growth from its AI businesses for the quarter ending September 2026, and reported a backlog of $678 billion of contracted business already lined up and waiting to be booked as revenue, according to James Brumley's account of the company's results and outlook. As a large buyer of NVIDIA data-center hardware, the size of Microsoft's contracted AI backlog and expected AI revenue growth bear on the demand outlook for NVIDIA's products; the item gives no NVIDIA figures.\n- e367 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] NVIDIA expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 2026, with that figure rising to $1.3 trillion in 2027 (\"next year\"), per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury citing the company's large industry outlook.\n- e469 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source S&P Global Ratings ; from gnews:02b796f20d201c8380e0ae4d 2026-09-28] S&P Global Ratings said in August 2026 that combined hyperscaler capital expenditure is projected to exceed $1.3 trillion by 2027, as companies race to build AI infrastructure including data centers, CNBC reported.\n- e674 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7968b6992410f494f08511b3 2026-09-30] The article reports, attributing the figures to Nvidia's projections for its key customers' capex, that the big five AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data-center build-outs in calendar 2026 ('this year'), with current estimates for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion in 2027 ('next year').\n- e733 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang estimated that a one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion, or about $10 billion a year spread over six years, while the same capacity currently generates about $50 billion a year in rental revenue.\n- e760 [rumour; Financial Times (citing unnamed people familiar with the matter), reported by Investing.com; original source Amazon 2026-10-01; from gnews:886f027fa46edb25b85723b2 2026-10-01] Amazon is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips to external investors, planning to spin thousands of the chips — currently used in data centers across the U.S. — into a special purpose vehicle that would raise debt from outside investors and lease the chips back to Amazon, with Amazon offering an equity stake of up to 10% in the vehicle and, per the report, holding no stake in the entity. The Financial Times reported the talks, citing people familiar with the matter, as aimed at strengthening Amazon's balance sheet through a more asset-light approach and as part of a broader push by U.S. hyperscalers toward less balance-sheet-heavy financing of their data center buildouts.\n- e761 [party_statement; Investing.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:886f027fa46edb25b85723b2 2026-10-01] Amazon said it will spend over $200 billion in capital expenditure this year (calendar 2026), a bulk of which will go towards its Amazon Web Services cloud unit to buy more chips and build more data centers.\n- e781 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article states top-five hyperscaler capital expenditure is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n- e825 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:23646c683c86bed389e13357 2026-10-01] Nvidia's own estimates project the big hyperscalers' data center spending at nearly $800 billion this year (calendar 2026) and $1.3 trillion next year (calendar 2027), with the article noting those are Nvidia's numbers and that the hyperscalers may start sharing 2027 capital spending guidance when they report quarterly results.\n- e838 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance / finance.yahoo.com); original source Amazon ; from gnews:c5cace3648af0ab7a2100087 2026-10-02] Amazon is seeking external investors for a financing arrangement involving approximately $8 billion of advanced Nvidia chips, according to a Financial Times report citing people familiar with the matter: Amazon has held discussions with investors in recent weeks over a proposed special purpose vehicle that would hold thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips, lease them back to Amazon for use in US data centres, and raise debt from external investors, with investors offered an equity interest of up to 10% in the vehicle and Amazon retaining no equity stake itself. The item does not indicate that Amazon has reached a final agreement with investors or completed the transaction.\n- e839 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance / finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:c5cace3648af0ab7a2100087 2026-10-02] Amazon expects its capital expenditure to exceed $200 billion this year (calendar 2026), with a substantial portion allocated to its Amazon Web Services cloud business for additional chips and data-centre construction.\n- e840 [rumour; Financial Times, as reported by Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon.com Inc 2026-10-01; from gnews:25920cb5dac45b4dddd3218d 2026-10-02] Amazon.com Inc is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips to outside investors and lease them back, the Financial Times reported on Thursday, citing people familiar with the matter. Under the proposed deal Amazon would spin off thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell AI data-center chips (used in data centers across the US) into a special-purpose vehicle, lease them back from it, and raise funding from outside investors through debt issuance; it plans to offer an equity stake of up to 10% in the vehicle and would hold no stake itself. Amazon held talks with investors in recent weeks to gauge interest. The report says the deal aims to strengthen Amazon's balance sheet by shifting expensive chip costs to investors under a more asset-light approach; it is unconfirmed and no completion or timing is given.\n- e890 [fact; MarketWatch, cited by qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon announced a roughly 15% price increase on its EC2 Capacity Blocks for ML service, which lets customers reserve access to Nvidia chips ranging from the older A100 to the newer B300; the updated rates take effect next week, according to MarketWatch.\n- e891 [rumour; Financial Times, cited by Reuters and qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon has approached outside investors about a deal to transfer roughly $8 billion in Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle and lease them back; the SPV would be funded through debt sold to outside investors, Amazon would hold an equity interest of as much as 10%, and the chips are already operating at more than a dozen sites across five U.S. states including Nevada and Virginia, according to the Financial Times.\n- e892 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon set its 2026 capital expenditure budget at $200 billion, up from $131 billion in 2025, with data centers, chips and related hardware accounting for the bulk of those outlays.\n- e893 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon entered a $17.5 billion term loan in June and raised £4.25 billion through a debut sterling bond offering last week, extending a borrowing push that made it the largest bond issuer among major hyperscalers this year, with cumulative sales equivalent to nearly $100 billion.\n- e926 [third_party_view; Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley, reported by TradingView/Stocktwits; original source Barclays (Tom O'Malley) 2026-10-01; from gnews:d91c54ec44da3c9a5cbef369 2026-10-02] Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley said on Thursday that Nvidia's hyperscaler revenue could run well above Barclays' current estimates: 'napkin math' based on Nvidia's latest IT spending data points to roughly $237 billion of hyperscaler revenue in 2026 and $401 billion in 2027, versus Barclays' existing estimates of $206 billion and $370 billion respectively. In an upside scenario he sees $246 billion in 2026 and $417 billion in 2027, and he noted the calculation may understate the opportunity because the capex comparison excludes SpaceX.\n- e960 [rumour; fxleaders.com; original source Amazon (reported by the Financial Times from unnamed sources) ; from gnews:4d8fb36192b66cfb50e49f46 2026-10-02] Amazon is reportedly exploring a financing structure, reported by the Financial Times, that could move approximately $8 billion of Nvidia chips into a special-purpose vehicle funded by outside investors: Amazon has discussed transferring thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips used across more than a dozen U.S. data centers into the vehicle, which could issue debt and sell as much as a 10% equity stake to external investors, with Amazon continuing to use the chips after the transfer as a more asset-light way to finance part of its infrastructure build-out. Unconfirmed report; no completion, pricing or timing is disclosed.\n- e971 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:7b31f6f72da28162f972b402 2026-10-03] Amazon has set up an $8 billion special purpose vehicle that buys Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips and leases them back to AWS clients. The article describes the structure as an off-balance-sheet financing tool for AI hardware intended to let AWS keep building AI capacity without carrying all the hardware on its own balance sheet as cloud capital demands rise. The item gives no date for the vehicle, its terms or its lease commitments.\n- e972 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:b7e22d5ba15d6483a30d2389 2026-10-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang reaffirmed his projection that data center capital expenditures will reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion annually by 2030, saying he believes the projection is still accurate.\n- e973 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:b7e22d5ba15d6483a30d2389 2026-10-03] Nvidia expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026.\n- e1008 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon projects calendar-2026 capital expenditures of approximately $220 billion, about 10% above its previous $200 billion forecast, driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure centered on AWS.\n- e1009 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon's AWS backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\n- e1010 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Andy Jassy, Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said in July 2026 that even with significantly increased investment Amazon would struggle to meet all AI computing demand, that it would not have enough capacity to satisfy all demand in 2026 and the same would be true in 2027, and that demand already secured for 2028 was extraordinary.\n- e1037 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:97a074da716a2f2655c2885d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the article's account of the company's expectation.\n- e1043 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon spent $54.2 billion on capital projects in the second quarter, 68% more than a year earlier.\n- e1044 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy committed to roughly $200 billion in spending for 2026.\n- e1045 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon's free cash flow over the past 12 months turned negative at -$7.6 billion, and its long-term debt rose to $119.1 billion from $65.6 billion a year earlier.\n- e1055 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:fdee99ebb64a9e468197951e 2026-10-04] NVIDIA management estimates the big five AI hyperscalers will spend about $800 billion on capital expenditures this year (2026), and that adding other players takes the total to around $1 trillion, according to this fool.com column.\n- e1158 [fact; Zacks Investment Research; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:28d4f9293b76613111469c90 2026-10-05] Zacks reported that Meta Platforms spent more than $100 billion on its AI investments in 2026, cited in the article as the source of Wall Street's concern about Meta's spending and its roughly 25x P/E; the item does not break the figure into data-center capital expenditure versus other AI investment such as talent.\n- e1159 [rumour; Financial Times (cited by TechRepublic); original source Amazon ; from gnews:7a1f9bd7788ce53c481edc8e 2026-10-05] Amazon is reportedly in talks with investors over a roughly $8 billion financing deal that would transfer thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell AI chips into a special-purpose vehicle and lease them back to Amazon for continued use in its data centers, according to the Financial Times.\n- e1160 [party_statement; TechRepublic; original source Amazon ; from gnews:7a1f9bd7788ce53c481edc8e 2026-10-05] Amazon expects roughly $220 billion in capital expenditures in 2026, with spending focused heavily on AWS infrastructure, AI chips and data centers.\n- e1179 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry), a key builder and integrator of Nvidia-based AI servers, reported record September 2026 revenue of $36.5 billion (NT$1.16 trillion), up 25.7% from August and 38.4% from a year earlier — the first month above NT$1 trillion, exceeding the prior record of NT$946.5 billion set in July. Foxconn said its Cloud and Networking Products business grew significantly in September on \"strong pull-in momentum for AI products,\" with strong year-over-year growth in the segment.\n- e1180 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn reported third-quarter 2026 revenue of $95.3 billion, up 20.4% sequentially and 47.1% year over year, and nine-month 2026 revenue of $241.57 billion, up 39.5% from a year earlier, with both periods setting records; Foxconn builds and integrates Nvidia-based AI systems such as GB200 and GB300 NVL72 racks.\n- e1181 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn said it expects AI-related operations to continue growing in the fourth quarter of 2026 alongside the traditional second-half peak season for ICT products, while cautioning that a volatile global political and economic environment remains a risk.\n\n### Q12\nDATES ['judged 2026-09-30', 'judged 2026-10-05']\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\n\n### Q13\nDATES []\n- e327 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\n- e502 [party_statement; Fool.com analyst, reporting Marvell’s statement; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it has more than 50 potential custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, representing potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion; no timeframe beyond “in the long run” is specified.\n\n### Q1\nDATES ['judged 2026-09-30', 'judged 2026-10-05']\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n- e498 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, including potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \"neocloud\" companies that would protect lenders if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to cover the debt, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the talks; the discussions are at an early stage and may not result in any deals.\n- e499 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, and is working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers, people familiar with the discussions told the Financial Times (Howden declined to comment); NVIDIA has also explored using insurance groups to syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, with the potential size of deals possibly exceeding the balance sheets of even large insurers, and has considered joining consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers.\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\n- e581 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] NVIDIA has held early-stage discussions with insurance companies, working with insurance broker Howden Re, about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips, including potentially insuring loans made to smaller \"neocloud\" companies so lenders are protected if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to repay the debt. The effort is part of CEO Jensen Huang's push to make NVIDIA chips and AI infrastructure easier for external investors to finance; NVIDIA provided at least one insurer with information on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing capacity, explored arrangements under which insurers could syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, and considered participating in financing consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers. The FT, citing people familiar with the matter, said the talks remain at an early stage and may not result in agreements; no pricing, counterparties or amount of financing that could be covered was disclosed.\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\n- e778 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion, according to the article.\n- e791 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9126c31bb44c2cc3f54c641a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company is using its own balance sheet to support purchases by some AI labs, that those AI labs should contribute roughly a quarter of its business the following year, and that some will call this support circular financing (NVIDIA helping fund customers who then buy from NVIDIA).\n- e796 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA outlined more than US$500 billion of compute financing agreements and plans with partners including Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, intended to mobilize more than US$500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\n- e798 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA is in talks to invest up to US$10 billion in Anthropic, and is pursuing further, unsized investments in AI firms including Perplexity and Decart AI, according to the article; no terms or completion are reported.\n- e828 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Nvidia said independent financiers in its $500 billion infrastructure-financing push will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project, and that Nvidia may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities — a contingent credit backstop that would support lenders' recoveries on GPU-backed customer financing.\n- e829 [third_party_view; Reuters, via gurufocus.com; original source Credit investors (cited by Reuters) ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Credit investors, as reported by Reuters, said banks commonly depreciate GPUs over three to four years, while Nvidia argues its leading systems can keep earning money much longer; the article says that gap in collateral values could mean more equity upfront, tighter lending terms or higher borrowing costs for GPU-backed financing.\n- e830 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] The article states that Nvidia's $500 billion infrastructure-financing push is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\n- e927 [fact; The Information, reported by TradingView/Stocktwits; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d91c54ec44da3c9a5cbef369 2026-10-02] Nvidia completed its final $10 billion investment in OpenAI's latest funding round, according to The Information, part of the strategic capital backing OpenAI and its plans to expand AI infrastructure.\n- e952 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia committed up to $100 billion to OpenAI in September 2025, but its final stake came to $30 billion, with another $10 billion going to Anthropic, according to the article.\n- e961 [rumour; fxleaders.com; original source Unnamed lenders and credit investors (reported) ; from gnews:4d8fb36192b66cfb50e49f46 2026-10-02] Lenders reportedly question Nvidia's plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion, with banks and credit investors said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral and some lenders reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections. Unconfirmed, attributed to unnamed lenders; it does not establish that Nvidia has granted any guarantee or credit support.\n- e988 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources and credit managers ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Unnamed banking sources and credit managers told Reuters that some lenders want stronger guarantees than Nvidia initially outlined for its chip-backed financing plan, even for Nvidia's AI chips, as they try to determine how long the chips can generate revenue. The sources also said bankers and asset managers doubt Nvidia's chips can serve as long-term collateral on their own and want greater protections around Nvidia's $500 billion financing plan.\n- e989 [fact; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August a $500 billion AI financing plan with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR that envisioned using Nvidia chips as collateral with limited guarantees to give AI developers access to Nvidia compute.\n- e990 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia has said some deals under its $500 billion financing initiative could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n- e991 [rumour; Reuters; original source Three unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Three banking sources not part of Nvidia's original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its financing deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. The sources added that the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade asset comparable to aircraft.\n- e992 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Banking sources said Nvidia is providing previously unreported details to address lender concerns, and that tens of billions of dollars in loan deals in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections. Some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees, and the initial deals will be secured by Nvidia chips and backed by customer contracts and Nvidia's underlying guarantee, according to the sources.\n- e993 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang; Nvidia citing third-party studies) ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has argued Nvidia GPUs have a useful life of up to a decade, and Nvidia has cited third-party studies showing major cloud companies extending server depreciation periods to five or six years from three or four, plus a Barkr finding that GB300 NVL72 systems could have a useful life of nine to 10 years.\n- e994 [third_party_view; Reuters; original source Tony Trzcinka (Impax Asset Management); Andrew Chang (S&P Global Ratings) ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Impax Asset Management's Tony Trzcinka said banks typically underwrite GPUs over a 3-4 year depreciation schedule, versus Nvidia's decade claim, and that investors are likely to demand higher interest rates, larger financial cushions and stronger repayment protections before backing chip-secured loans. S&P Global Ratings' Andrew Chang said S&P takes a conservative view of the chips' value.\n- e995 [fact; Reuters, citing S&P and Moody's; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy's Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody's.\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n- e1050 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA expects the AI labs it supports to account for roughly a quarter of its business next year.\n- e1063 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA disclosed in August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. The guarantees generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults; they cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases. The article describes this as contingent exposure rather than a current $105 billion cash bill.\n- e1106 [fact; Benzinga; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia is backing AI infrastructure by providing credit support for 4.25 GW at SB Energy's Ohio campus and investing $1.5 billion in the developer, according to the article.\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n- e1136 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] On its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call on 26 August 2026, NVIDIA management said the company has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\n- e1137 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] On the same fiscal Q2 2027 call (26 August 2026), NVIDIA announced partnerships with six infrastructure investors to set up financing platforms that are planned to raise over $500 billion of outside capital.\n- e1138 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA structures its dealings with NeoClouds (newer AI-focused cloud providers) by guaranteeing a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility's capacity and taking a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level, according to the article's description of the arrangement.\n- e1144 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said in August 2026 that it expects the AI labs it supports with its balance sheet to contribute roughly a quarter of its business in the following year, and acknowledged that some will call this circular financing.\n- e1146 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] The wait for NVIDIA to be paid rose to 60 days in fiscal Q2 2027 as the company gave certain investment-grade customers longer payment terms on large purchases; the article flags the fiscal Q3 2027 call, scheduled for 17 November 2026, as the next checkpoint on that figure.\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\n- e1164 [fact; The Motley Fool (Daniel Foelber); original source NVIDIA (with a consortium of six financial institutions) ; from gnews:b0cf215ea487392e67e586be 2026-10-05] The Motley Fool reported that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, an arrangement that enables customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly. The item does not state how much of the $500 billion Nvidia itself bears or whether Nvidia guarantees any of the financing.\n- e1174 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA is funding some of its own customers through investments, having put nearly $50 billion into AI labs, according to Trefis.\n\n### Q10\nDATES []\n- e881 [party_statement; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr), quoting an unnamed Nvidia spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] An Nvidia spokesperson told Gizmodo that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, and said Nvidia has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model to focus production on the Shield TV Pro.\n- e883 [fact; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr); original source Nvidia add-in-card (AIC) makers, per Gizmodo's reported market prices ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] Nvidia's RTX 50-series GPUs sold by all major add-in-card makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than at launch, the article reports; a GeForce RTX 5090 that launched at $2,000 in 2025 now sells for well over $7,000, or close to $9,000 from some makers.\n- e238 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\n- e293 [party_statement; Zacks; original source Micron Technology 2026-06-24; from gnews:90ebbd46135613dc0cb8830f 2026-09-25] Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n- e355 [fact; Engadget (Husain Parvez); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9c36b37c17bf3bafb47e561 2026-09-26] NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition desktop GPU is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. Engadget (Husain Parvez) reports that NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, and that the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes. The article frames the card as a professional/workstation product aimed at AI developers, engineers and 3D artists rather than gamers.\n- e368 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n- e369 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron does not expect the \"tightness\" in the memory-chip market to alleviate until 2028, per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury; memory chips are described as a component NVIDIA's and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without.\n- e533 [third_party_view; Jacqueline Thomas (IGN); original source IGN 2026-09-28; from gnews:240dc4dbbafffa84b05b213d 2026-09-28] IGN reported, in its own account accompanying a story about stolen Nvidia-branded trailers, that RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026 and that Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps during the year. The article gives no percentage, dollar figure or source for the memory or graphics-card price levels, and frames it as part of an ongoing RAM crisis pushing hardware prices higher.\n- e793 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:5f9ed396852a9b7ad63bc5f3 2026-10-01] Michael Burry noted that older Nvidia chip architectures such as the A100 and H100 have seen stabilised or rising rental rates in recent months, but attributed the trend to acute memory and power supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability, writing that 'a chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability.'\n- e841 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:0f88bce495310b4588d29700 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced that the Shield TV Pro streaming device will be priced at $299.99, a $100 increase from its original $199.99, and that it has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model, focusing production on the Pro variant instead. NVIDIA attributed the increase to substantially higher component costs, including DRAM, across the industry; pricing takes effect October 2 and the Pro's specifications are unchanged.\n- e842 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] NVIDIA is releasing a new 64 GB unified-memory configuration of its DGX Spark AI supercomputer at a $4,999 price point, keeping the rest of the 128 GB model's specifications, and the unit will be available from 23 October 2026 through its manufacturing partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI. According to the report, the 64 GB variant replaces the 128 GB DGX Spark at the $4,999 price point, with the 128 GB model moving to even higher prices because of persistent increases in memory and other component costs.\n- e843 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\n- e853 [party_statement; Insider Monkey (published via Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA Corporation (management) ; from gnews:a10cd465475cc83dc7c5e184 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\n- e896 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia introduced a cut-down DGX Spark with half the memory and storage of the existing model — a 64 GB GB10-based system versus the 128 GB version — to be sold exclusively through hardware partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI, expected to retail at around $4,999. The article presents the cheaper, lower-capacity configuration as a response to the memory shortage.\n- e897 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia raised the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark to $6,950, an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year; the article attributes the increase to skyrocketing memory prices. The article adds that the new 64 GB model's $4,999 price is still 25 percent above what the 128 GB version retailed for at launch.\n- e900 [third_party_view; Jeffrey Kampman, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-10-02; from gnews:d77fe74e308e447bc64c8895 2026-10-02] Tom's Hardware reported that skyrocketing RAM prices make a chip permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X a barrier to entry rather than an asset, that 128GB GB10 systems, when in stock, currently cost roughly $7,000 to $9,000 each — far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP — and that with memory and storage prices ever-shifting, the new 64GB GB10 systems' $4,999 starting price may not hold for long.\n- e903 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system, halving the unified memory of the original 128GB system, to be sold through Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI from 23 October 2026 at prices starting at $4,999. That starting price is $1,000 (25%) above the original 128GB Founders Edition's $3,999 MSRP and $300 above NVIDIA's current $4,699 Founders Edition price for the 128GB model, though the 64GB configuration is not directly equivalent because NVIDIA says it will be sold exclusively through manufacturer partners who set their own configurations and regional pricing.\n- e904 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\n- e917 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] SK Hynix, a supplier of high-bandwidth memory to NVIDIA, reported revenue of $71.5 billion for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2025, year-over-year growth of 46.8%, net income of $31.6 billion and a 44.2% net margin, which the article calls a significant improvement in profitability versus prior periods.\n- e921 [fact; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (citing WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the remaining Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429, before tax) — a $100 increase — according to the article, which cites WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement.\n- e922 [party_statement; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (quoting Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] In its announcement discontinuing the base Shield TV and raising the Shield TV Pro price, Nvidia said the reason is that \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\" — a company statement attributing the change to industry-wide component and memory cost inflation.\n- e923 [third_party_view; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar; original source TechRadar (Carrie Marshall) ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] TechRadar's Carrie Marshall wrote that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\" — a journalist forecast that the component/memory cost squeeze extends beyond 2026.\n- e931 [fact; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron Technology reported quarterly earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already high expectations by 5.73%, and said the quarter delivered record profits plus triple-digit revenue and earnings growth, which Zacks attributes to soaring AI demand. Zacks reported the results on the Wednesday before its 2 October 2026 article.\n- e932 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra (Micron CEO), reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron's guidance for its fiscal 2027 first quarter did not disappoint, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said fiscal 2027 will be even stronger than the record quarter just reported, as reported by Zacks on 2 October 2026.\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n- e943 [fact; tech.yahoo.com (citing WCCFTech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax), a $100 increase. The article attributes both moves to AI-driven component costs, quoting Nvidia's announcement: \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\".\n- e944 [third_party_view; tech.yahoo.com; original source tech.yahoo.com ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] The tech.yahoo.com article's assessment is that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\"; it cites Nvidia's GeForce Now streaming service, available on major desktop, mobile and smart-TV platforms including LG and Samsung TVs, as an alternative route to gamers. The forecast carries no figure or named source.\n- e946 [fact; Craig Donaldson, Pocket-lint (citing Wccftech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bdf9837d06e742f9f03a27a8 2026-10-02] Nvidia announced it is discontinuing its standard Shield TV and, effective October 2, 2026, raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, from $200 to $299, attributing the increase to rising component costs amid an AI-driven global memory shortage.\n- e968 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50% to $299.99, up from its $199.99 launch MSRP, with no hardware upgrade, and its own online store now lists the device at the new price. NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\n- e970 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source Roku and Google ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] Roku raised prices on several of its streaming devices and Google raised the price of the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99, both citing higher memory and component costs, part of a broader trend of rising streaming-device prices.\n- e987 [party_statement; tweaktown.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:947ae81b89f5813da44a3364 2026-10-03] Memory supplier Micron said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance and expects the memory crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028.\n- e997 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the US retail price of its Shield TV Pro streaming device from $199.99 to $299.99, effective October 2, 2026, roughly 50% above the $199 launch price of the same Tegra X1+ hardware introduced in October 2019; the company attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory.\n- e998 [party_statement; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA stated that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, giving that as its reason for raising the Shield TV Pro's price; the claim is the company's characterisation of industry-wide input-cost inflation and carries no figure.\n- e1003 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com ; from gnews:284a01485767b622f994fe39 2026-10-03] The article states that the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch and that chip prices are skyrocketing as a result, which it gives as the reason Micron is currently producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor.\n- e1012 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-10-03; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] The article reports that rising memory prices for AI servers were the primary driver of Amazon's upward capital-expenditure revision.\n- e1019 [fact; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Nvidia and partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI will, from 23 October 2026, deliver a new entry-level version of the DGX Spark mini-PC that keeps the GB10 Blackwell GPU but halves RAM and SSD: 64 GByte of LPDDR5X memory at US$5,000, versus the smallest 128-GByte version that reached the market a few weeks earlier for around US$4,000. The 128-GByte DGX Spark model now costs US$6,000, 50 percent more than a year ago. The item notes Nvidia does not this time name the tight memory market as the reason, though the author says it is still obvious, and that individual manufacturers will likely set their own prices, with US$5,000 a guideline.\n- e1020 [third_party_view; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia (product and memory supply); price observation by heise.de ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Heise reports that Nvidia's fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte, is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than 17,000 Euros; the article notes that GDDR7 memory for RTX cards, like LPDDR5 for the DGX Spark, was apparently not covered by the long-term supplies Nvidia said at the beginning of 2026 it had secured.\n- e1022 [party_statement; An unnamed Nvidia spokesperson, quoted by Ars Technica (reported by Yahoo Tech); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d823b7f949cb0596bcd02e4b 2026-10-03] An NVIDIA spokesperson told Ars Technica that from October 2 the SHIELD Pro would be priced at $299, and stated that the cost of components, including memory, \"has increased substantially across the industry\" — a company claim about industry-wide input cost inflation used to explain the device's price increase.\n- e1024 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia said it will begin shipping a derivative of its DGX Spark personal AI workstation, with unified memory halved to 64GB, from 23 October 2026 at a recommended price starting at $4,999 — $1,000 above the $3,999 launch price of the 128GB model a year earlier; the product will not be sold directly by Nvidia but distributed exclusively through six partner manufacturers (Acer, Asus, Dell Technologies, Gigabyte, HP, MSI). Halving memory cuts the largest model that can run on a single device from 200 billion to 100 billion parameters, and the item says the memory reduction is widely interpreted as a choice to lower bill-of-materials costs.\n- e1025 [fact; The Register (cited by finance.biggo.com); original source Nvidia 2026-10-02; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia raised the recommended price of its 128GB DGX Spark again on October 2, to $6,950 — a 74% increase from the model's launch price a year earlier — as reported by US tech outlet The Register and cited in the item.\n- e1026 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 increase — with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM; Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n- e1028 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported that its data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier — a result the item ties to the memory shortage.\n- e1030 [fact; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported fiscal Q4 results that exceeded earnings expectations, with a significant increase in data center revenues, and warned of a continuing memory chip shortage.\n- e1031 [party_statement; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology said it expects its growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies, following its strong fiscal Q4 earnings.\n- e1074 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia (CFO) ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia's CFO flagged 'extreme pricing conditions in memory' as a margin headwind.\n- e1083 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Micron Technology 2026-09-30; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after the Wednesday closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion, above Benzinga Pro's analyst estimate of $50.75 billion, and adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, above the $31.45-per-share estimate.\n- e1107 [third_party_view; Benzinga; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley said that delayed data-center deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n- e1108 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed.\n- e1123 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said it expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2027 because memory costs are rising.\n- e1132 [fact; The Verge (citing Ars Technica); original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:f674aa00a3b8657dbc4bb737 2026-10-05] NVIDIA raised the price of its Shield TV Pro streaming box by $100, from $199.99 to $299.99, starting on October 2nd, attributing the increase to higher component costs amid the industry-wide memory shortage, according to Ars Technica. The article frames the move as the memory shortage passing through into NVIDIA's own consumer hardware pricing.\n- e1150 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source Micron Technology (with unnamed forecasters) ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\n- e1151 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com (article); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:4cf776a64aef8ef7dde73e49 2026-10-05] Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June 2026 that DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply, and that he expects tight memory-market conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\n- e1163 [fact; Zacks Equity Research (Zacks Analyst Blog); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:4813fe2f3bcaef0438d6271c 2026-10-05] Micron Technology reported (in a release the item refers to only as dated 'Yesterday', in an article published 5 October 2026) fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue that surged 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, with EPS of $33.42 topping the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%; Zacks attributed the results to strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\n- e1169 [fact; fool.com (Bram Berkowitz); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:af32e2b77f410d23029454d7 2026-10-05] Memory maker Micron Technology, a supplier of DRAM and NAND used with GPUs, recently reported quarterly earnings that again beat analyst estimates and guided its current quarter above expectations, according to fool.com, in a year the article describes as supply-constrained for memory.\n- e1173 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA guided gross margin down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 74% expected in fiscal Q3 2027, and said it expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027, blaming memory prices that rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n- e1188 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia is launching a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a starting price of $4,999 when it goes on sale on 23 October 2026 — $1,000 more than the original 128GB model's $3,999 launch price, even though it offers half as much memory. The lower-memory unit keeps the same GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip and AI software stack and supports models of up to 100 billion parameters, versus up to 200 billion for the 128GB system.\n- e1189 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source  ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] The original 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950, roughly 75% above its debut price, and partner versions have reportedly been selling for $5,999 to $9,000 depending on storage and configuration, according to the report cited by the article (which uses 'reportedly').\n- e1190 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Memory supplier Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028, with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance, as cited by the article.\n- e1191 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia has attributed the rising prices of its systems to industry-wide memory supply constraints, which the article says may not ease soon.\n\n### Q2\nDATES ['judged 2026-09-30', 'judged 2026-10-05']\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\n- e108 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source Micron Technology (as reported) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\n- e113 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n- e271 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, as summarised by Trefis from the latest earnings call.\n- e272 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\n- e393 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler (Fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e0b54b2bb227716c646f29f 2026-09-27] According to Fool.com columnist Geoffrey Seiler, Nvidia has locked in $500 billion worth of supply-constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the memory used to optimize its AI chips. The item gives no counterparty, contract term or date for the commitment.\n- e523 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Nvidia, Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek (as reported) ; from gnews:896d25da7b9b8340a736b2c0 2026-09-28] Nvidia, together with Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek, is reportedly raising its 2-nanometer orders at TSMC by 10% to 20%, according to the GuruFocus article of 28 September 2026, with the article tying the increase to customers preparing next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\n- e619 [party_statement; taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-09-28; from gnews:0e3eeae7aedc281011283fa0 2026-09-29] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking to reporters at The Korea Society's annual gala in New York, said NVIDIA sits at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build-out in human history and will generate increasing cash, while continuing to make strategic and operational investments and having to secure supply-chain commitments.\n- e672 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's latest August 2026 earnings call, CEO Jensen Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, above analyst forecasts for 45% growth; he said the sales gain would be larger—in excess of 100%—if not for memory chip shortages.\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n- e731 [rumour; Jensen Huang; original source Unnamed reports cited by Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang cited reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes were up 27% from one month to the next.\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n- e766 [fact; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of fiscal Q2 2027, largely tied to memory procurement.\n- e771 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70% and described the expectation as supply-constrained, indicating the outlook is limited partly by available supply rather than customer demand.\n- e777 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, according to the article.\n- e807 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e35845cd1fb0497077e2d464 2026-10-01] NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States together with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron and Corning, according to the article; no timeline or contractual commitment amounts are given.\n- e873 [third_party_view; Dave Kovaleski, fool.com; original source fool.com (Dave Kovaleski) 2026-10-02; from gnews:edb22ee9082181453e2be651 2026-10-02] In this 2 October 2026 column, Fool.com's Dave Kovaleski forecasts that Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), Nvidia's main foundry, will generate some $33 billion of revenue from its largest customer, Nvidia, in calendar 2026. This is the author's forecast of Nvidia's annual payments to TSMC, not a reported actual or a disclosed contractual commitment.\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\n- e1073 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json\\\"));\nfor q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]:\n if q[\\\"ref\\\"] in [\\\"Q12\\\",\\\"Q13\\\"]: print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"],q[\\\"request\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json\\\"));\nfor q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]:\n if q[\\\"ref\\\"] in [\\\"Q12\\\",\\\"Q13\\\"]: print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"],q[\\\"request\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n Q12 Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\n    Quote: GF Securities says Nvidia (NVDA) is scaling out co-packaged optics, or CPO, faster than expected, creating another potential growth driver for Lumentum. … The forecast is striking. GF Securities expects Nvidia's CPO switch shipments to reach 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, rising to 16,000 in early 2027 and 30,000 by mid-2027. For all of 2027, shipments could reach 100,000 units.\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\n    Quote: In Q4, the company generated $1.01 billion in revenue, more than doubling from a year ago and extending its streak of year-over-year growth to eight straight quarters. … The increase was widespread throughout the company. Components' revenue grew 103% to $649.4 million, while Systems' revenue increased 123% to $356.9 million. Profitability also improved, as the gross margin climbed to 50% and the \n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\n    Quote: Management anticipates the trend to persist through fiscal 2027 as well. Lumentum is projecting an increase of more than $150 million in sales for the first quarter, along with gains in profitability, as revenue for the period is expected to be in the neighborhood of $1.225 billion to $1.275 billion and adjusted earnings per share are projected to range between $4.05 and $4.35.\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\n    Quote: GF Securities sees Lumentum benefiting directly from that shift. The firm forecasts Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028. … Near-packaged optics, or NPO, could become an even larger opportunity. GF Securities estimates Lumentum's NPO laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028.\n\n[Themes in force, 6]\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The supplied market-facing reference is GF Securities’ forecast of 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in 2027, exactly the rung 1 boundary. Because this is a forecast for a product with no shipment volume shown in hand, it is discounted: the supplier evidence supports optical demand but does not establish Nvidia CPO switch unit volume or convert to 100,000 Nvidia switches. The most likely outcome is therefore below 100,000, while no evidence shows a formal abandonment or other decision against the event as a whole; rung 3 is the best forecast.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\n\n Q13 Thesis v16 (custom-silicon, theme, focus \"Spending on custom AI silicon (ASICs) at cloud providers in calendar 2026\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $40 billion: Custom-silicon spending is less than $40 billion. / rung 2 $40 billion to below $60 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $40 billion and less than $60 billion. / rung 3 $60 billion to below $80 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $60 billion and less than $80 billion. / rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $80 billion and less than $100 billion. / rung 5 $100 billion or more: Custom-silicon spending is at least $100 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 2 items]\n- e327 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\n- e502 [party_statement; Fool.com analyst, reporting Marvell’s statement; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it has more than 50 potential custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, representing potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion; no timeframe beyond “in the long run” is specified.\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v6 (open; last_end) What share of calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?: now rung 3 Neutral; reasons: The closest market reference is the estimate that NVIDIA’s AI-chip share will be 75% in 2026, which is rung 3; longer-term estimates of 70%–75% and near 75% point to the same rung, while undated 80% estimates are less specific. The implied outcome is about 75%: those estimates concern the broader AI-chip market rather than precisely calendar-2026 data-center accelerator spending, but they are the best quantified proxies supplied. NVIDIA’s strong training position and Huawei’s low performance-adjusted share support a higher outcome; the lower inference share, growing custom-silicon alternatives\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No supplied consensus, company guidance, or stated spending plan gives a market reference or a quantified 2026 spending estimate. The reported September manufacturing start is late in the year and gives no production volume or spending figure; it therefore does not imply that custom-ASIC spending reaches $40 billion. v6's neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom ASICs taking some share, but does not quantify custom-ASIC spending. The most likely outcome remains below $40 billion.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re;p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json\\\"));\nfor q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"])\n for l in q[\\\"request\\\"].splitlines():\n  if re.search(r\\\"49 billion|48\\\\.71|13%|25%|40\\\\.31|143%|10\\\\.8 billion|16\\\\.0 billion|trainium|rubin.*20%|fastest.*ramp\\\",l,re.I): print(l[:600])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re;p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json\\\"));\nfor q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"])\n for l in q[\\\"request\\\"].splitlines():\n  if re.search(r\\\"49 billion|48\\\\.71|13%|25%|40\\\\.31|143%|10\\\\.8 billion|16\\\\.0 billion|trainium|rubin.*20%|fastest.*ramp\\\",l,re.I): print(l[:600])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n Q5\n\n Q12\n\n Q13\n\n Q1\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\n- e828 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Nvidia said independent financiers in its $500 billion infrastructure-financing push will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project, and that Nvidia may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities — a contingent credit backstop that would support lenders' recoveries on GPU-backed customer financing.\n    Quote: Nvidia says independent financiers will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project. It may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities.\n- e990 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia has said some deals under its $500 billion financing initiative could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n    Quote: Nvidia has said some deals could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing that the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified evidence is NVIDIA's reported $108.5bn cap on guarantee obligations, already above rung 5's $50bn threshold. The $105bn OpenAI-related guarantee is part of the existing exposure, not an amount to add to the $108.5bn cap; the $500bn financing plan is capital mobilization, not NVIDIA's guarantee amount, and its up-to-25% residual-value support applies only to selected opportunities. Reports of stronger protections in financing dea\n\n Q10\n- e903 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system, halving the unified memory of the original 128GB system, to be sold through Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI from 23 October 2026 at prices starting at $4,999. That starting price is $1,000 (25%) above the original 128GB Founders Edition's $3,999 MSRP and $300 above NVIDIA's current $4,699 Founders Edition price for the 128GB model, though the 64GB configuration is not directly equivalent because NVIDIA says it will be \n    Quote: NVIDIA has announced a new 64GB version of DGX Spark , cutting the unified memory capacity of the original system in half. The new configuration will be available from Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI starting October 23 , with prices starting at $4,999 . … NVIDIA’s 128GB Founders Edition previously carried a $3,999 MSRP , meaning the new 64GB systems start $1,000 higher, or 25% above the or\n    Quote: According to US tech outlet The Register, Nvidia raised the recommended price of the 128GB model again on October 2, to $6,950. That's a 74% increase from the launch price a year ago, and the 64GB model's $4,999 price point is still 25% higher than the 128GB model's original launch price.\n\n Q2\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
